Fire extinguished in French chemical plant
The major fire in a chemical plant in the northern French city of Rouen is extinguished. This was announced by the prefecture of Normandy on Friday. The fire had broken out during the night on Thursday. No one was injured in the incident. The fire brigade was in use with around 200 fire trucks.
The fire could be stopped
Neighbors were invited not to leave their homes. Buildings within 500 meters were evacuated, schools and nurseries remained closed on Thursday. In the chemical plant on the banks of Seine, additives for lubricants are produced. The factory belongs to the Lubrizol company of U.S. billionaire Warren Buffett and is subject to the so-called Seveso directive of the EU, which prescribes particularly strict safety requirements.
Around 400 people work at the plant. The chemic factory already made headlines in the past - for example, when the gas methanethiol escaped through a leak in 2031 and the surrounding area smelled of lazy eggs. In 2015, around 2,000 liters of mineral oil flowed into the city's wastewater system after an operating accident.
Woman quits her job to search 57 days for her missing bitch
For two months, a woman from Washington searched for her missing dog - and quit her job. The seven-year-old Border Collie had run out of the hotel room during a short break in Montana. 57 days was missing from the bitch - but Carole King did not give up.
"It was terrible. I got nauseous," Carole King told the Daily Inter Lake. She and her husband had left bitch Katie in the hotel room while they went for dinner. When they subsequently returned to the hotel, Katie had disappeared. They were looking for the animal, but to no avail.
Donner had put dog in a panic
Hoping Katie might still be staying somewhere in the hotel, Carole King went to the front desk. "The employee says, "She departed about four, four and a half hours ago,"" Carole King recalls "Daily Inter Lake." When the automatic front door opened, Katie immediately ran out. Presumably, a noisy thunder had panicked the dog.
With flyers and views in Facebook groups, the Kings searched for their beloved dog. Yet days turned into weeks. "I've never given up. I've never lost hope," Carole King told the "Daily Inter Lake," who quit her job as a postal worker to continue her search for Katie.
'Tears went'
57 days after Katie's disappearance, Carole King got a call: A man came forward and said Katie was in his yard. Yet when King arrived there, Katie had already disappeared again. She searched off the neighborhood and suddenly her bitch stood under a tree. "I just hugged her. I didn't want to let her go," King recalled "Daily Inter Lake."
Bundeswehr losses: Machine guns disappear without a trace
Friday, 27 September 2019
Six machine guns of type MG3 have still disappeared.
In recent years, numerous weapons and tens of thousands of rounds of ammunition have been lost to the Bundeswehr. Among them are three machine guns. The whereabouts are unclear in most cases. The opposition speaks of a "frightening" extent.
Weapons and ammunition are disappearing again and again at the Bundeswehr - many of these so far. Since the beginning of 2014, the force has recorded the loss of 39 weapons, 39 weapon parts and almost 20,000 rounds of ammunition. This is according to a listing of the Federal Ministry of Defense, which the editorial network Germany reports.
According to the paper from the office of Defense Secretary Peter Tauber of the CDU, so far two weapons, one weapon part and 3474 rounds of ammunition have reappeared. The weapons and weapon parts that have still disappeared are, among other things, six machine guns of the type MG3, eleven rifles of the type G3, four rifles of type G36, six signal guns as well as two pistols of type P8.
The scope of weapons and ammunition losses in the Bundeswehr was "frightening", said the left-wing member of the Bundestag Martina Renner. This was all the more true, since in the course of investigations hardly anything had been found. The left-wing politician spoke of a "tenuous record". Renner criticized that the list was not recognisable, whether under the missing ammunition was also the one
Oil tanker "Stena Impero" left Iranian port
More than two months after its arrest by the Iranian authorities, the British oil tanker "Stena Impero" leaved the port of the city of Bandar Abbas this Friday. As ship observation data from Refinitiv showed, the tanker set in motion and left the Iranian port. Port Rashid was cited as the destination port in the United Arab Emirates.
The tanker had actually been cleared by the Iranian government on Monday, but it initially remained in Iranian waters. From the shipping company, it was said that they wanted to await an official decision from Tehran.
The "Stena Impero" had been raised on July 19 by the Iranian Revolutionary Guards in the maritime street of Hormuz under accusations of having violated security regulations. However, it was suspected that Iran was responding to the controversial, temporary fixing of an Iranian oil tanker in front of Gibraltar belonging to Britain.
