Merkel pays tribute to people's courage at the Peaceful Revolution
German Chancellor Angela Merkel has honored the commitment of the people in the GDR who took to the streets 30 years ago during the Peaceful Revolution. In the autumn of 1989, courageous citizens would have shaken the then GDR regime, Merkel said at a ceremonial act of the Thüringer CDU faction on the imminent day of German Unity in Erfurt. The chancellor, among other things, reminded of the occupation
Musk aims for delivery record
Tesla's quarter potentially holds record. Despite delivery boom, there were recent losses at the electric car company.
New York.Tesla CEO Elon Musk has given his electric car company a strong boost in the stock market with the prospect of new record numbers. "We have the chance to reach our first quarter with 100,000 deliveries," Musk wrote in an e-mail to Tesla employees published by the industry blog "Electrek."
Demand is strong, so Tesla could place 110000 orders. The challenge is to bring the cars quickly to the customers. Tesla would have to offer as many resources as possible at the end of the quarter.
Tesla's shares went out of trading with an increase of over six percent. The price hasn't increased so sharply for almost four months. In the second quarter, Tesla had delivered a good 95000 cars and thus reached its previous record.
Musk needs records to achieve his high targets. In 2019, he wants to deliver a total of 360000 to 400000 cars. For this, six-figure figures have to be found in the current and next quarter year. Above all, the cheapest Tesla Model 3 is in demand, but here the profit margins are low. Despite the boom, there have been losses recently.
Riddle solved: That's why we can't tickle ourselves
A kind of brake in the cerebrum prevents people from tickling themselves. Scientists at the Humboldt University in Berlin found this out with the help of experiments on rats, as they announced on Thursday evening. Progress has also been made on the question of why children defend themselves so fiercely against tickling, only to demand more.
After all, it has also beemen shown why the approach of a tickling hand triggers laughter even before touch. This is due to a low-lying layer in the cerebrum, which already triggers the laughter at the expectation of tickling.
According to the information, previous research showed that rats react with "laughter" in the ultrasound range when they are tickled by humans. This "laughter" is mediated by the part of the cerebral cortex, which serves the central processing of haptic perception.
Laughter is suppressed during self-touching
Until now, it had been assumed that the brain could distinguish between self-touching and touch by others - i.e. that is why people cannot tickle themselves. However, the Berlin researchers now found out in the experiments on the rats that laughter as well as the activity of the respective area of the cerebral cortex was suppressed during a self-touch.
But if the self-touch and the touch by others happen at the same time, laughter is also suppressed. This suggests that the rat’s brain could not distinguish between self-touching and touch by others. The new findings suggested that people cannot tickle themselves, because the self-touching activates a brake in the cerebrum.
The rats were also trained to tickle themselves. In doing so, they sometimes broke off this initiation prematurely in the experiment, showed flight behaviors or frightening rigidity. This ambivalence resembles the behavior of the children. The ambivalence of tickling is a behavioral reaction that is the same in rats and people.
Disaster averted: Major fire extinguished at French chemical plant
The major fire in a chemical plant in the northern French city of Rouen is extinguished. This was announced by Normandie prefecture to the AFP news agency on Friday. The fire had broken out in the night to Thursday.
According to authorities, no hazardous substances are said to have leaked out. The samples taken give no reason to be disturbed, as Prefect Pierre-André Durand said at a press conference on Friday morning. No one was injured in the incident. The fire department was in use with around 200 firefighting vehicles.
Schools and nurseries have been closed
Local residents were urged not to leave their homes. Buildings within 500 meters were evacuated, schools and nurseries remained closed on Thursday.
Factory belongs to Lubrizol's Lubrizol company by U.S. Billionaire Warren Buffett
In the chemical plant on the banks of the Seine, additives for lubricants are produced. The factory belongs to the Lubrizol company of US billionaire Warren Buffett and is subject to the so-called Seveso directive of the EU, which prescribes particularly strict safety requirements. Around 400 people work in the plant.
The chemical factory already made headlines in the past - for example, when the methanethiol gas escaped through a leak in 2013 and the surrounding area smelled of lazy eggs. In 2015, around 2000 liters of mineral oil flowed into the city's wastewater system after an operational accident.
Brexit: Johnson criticised by own sister
The prime minister's sister commented on Boris Johnson's speech at the first session in parliament following the lifting of the forced pause on Wednesday evening. The prime minister threatens to lead the country out of the EU without an agreement on 31 October should Brussels fail to get involved in his demands for changes to the Brexit agreement. How he intends to achieve this, however, is unclear.
About Cox, who campaigned for Britain's residence in the European Union, the Premier said: "The best way to honor Jo Cox's memory and make this country one again would be the EU exit." Cox had been murdered by a right-wing extremist in 2016 during the referendum election campaign.
Memorandum to attacks of Oslo and Utöya is unveiled
More than eight years after the attacks in Oslo and Utöya with 77 deaths, a memorial will be unveiled in honor of the victims on Saturday in Norway. The monument "Jernrosene" (German: the iron roses) consists of around 1,000 iron rose replicas. They are reminiscent of the roses deposited by thousands of people in the days and weeks after the attacks in front of Oslo.
At the same location, the project of the artists Tobbe Malm and Tone Karlsrud is to be unveiled - the roses were forged by people from all over the world, including survivors and relatives of the victims. At the opening, among others, Crown Prince Haakon and Crown Princess Mette-Marit are expected.
Right-wing terrorist Anders Behring Breivik had initially detonated a car bomb in Oslo's government district on July 22, 2011 and subsequently shot dozens of participants in a holiday camp of the youth organization of the Social Democratic Workers' Party. A total of 77 people were killed in the attacks.
Iran releases British tankers
The U.N. general debate brought no solution to the Iran conflict. But now there's one less. Britain's "Stena Impero" oil tanker detained in Iran was able to set sail again. It could be a piece of a puzzle in the peace plan of Iran's President Rouhani.
The British oil tanker "Stena Impero", which has been held in the Strait of Hormuz in Iran since mid-July, is back in international waters. After being released by Iranian authorities, the ship is on its way to Dubai, according to Stena Bulk.
The "Stena Impero" has left the port of Bandar Abbas and the crew is now going to Dubai, among other things, for medical examinations, explained shipping chief Erik Hånell. At noon, the ship had left the Iranian waters. "It's nice that it's over," he told the Swedish television station SVT.
The Ministry of Foreign Affairs in London described the detention of the ship as illegal. "This is part of a pattern of attempts to impair the freedom of the seas. We are working with our international partners to maintain shipping and international law," it said in a statement on Friday.
The Iranian Revolutionary Guards had arrested the tanker on July 19 under the allegation to have disregarded regulations of maritime law in the Persian Gulf. Seven of the 23 crew members had already been released at the beginning of September. According to the remaining 16 crew members, Hånell said. They had behaved professionally in view of the difficult and exhausting situation in the past ten weeks. The shipping company is now working on
The British government suspected a response to a similar incident in British Gibraltar behind the fixing of the "Stena Impero": At the beginning of July, a supertanker with oil from Iran, which is said to have been destined for Syria, had been placed on the chain. According to the authorities, the delivery violated EU sanctions against the country of war Syria.
Iran spoke of "piracy" and threatened with consequences. In mid-August, the "Adrian Darya-1" was finally released. Suspections that it was a barter deal to clear the "Stena Impero," London vehemently dismissed.
The Strait of Hormuz is one of the world’s most important naval roads. It connects the Persian Gulf with the Indian Ocean. Much of the world’s oil export is shipped through the strait. Tensions in the region had intensified immensely in the past few months. Merchant shipping was dragged into the conflict between the U.S. and Iran. There were several incidents involving tankers and drones.
Behind the tensions between Tehran and Washington is the nuclear dispute of both countries. The Americans accuse the Iranian leadership of wanting to build nuclear weapons. Iran rejects this. The U.S. was single-handedly withdrawing from the international nuclear agreement in 2018, which should prevent Iran from building a nuclear bomb and at the same time end its political and economic isolation. Since then, the U. Government has put the leadership in Tehran with massive economic sanctions against the country
Due to the dispute over "Stena Impero" and other incidents involving tankers in the Persian Gulf and oil facilities in Saudi Arabia, the U.S. is working on a coalition for military protection of merchant ships in the region.
Iranian President Hassan Rouhani presented a plan for security and peace in the Strait of Hormuz at the U.N. General Assembly in New York. With the "Coalition of Hope" initiative for the Persian Gulf, Iran, together with the Arab Gulf states and without foreign protective powers, wants to ensure the safety of shipping in the Persian Gulf.
With its protectionist economic policy, India has a better future.
India would actually be predestined as a "war profiteer". In view of the punitive tariffs imposed by America's President Donald Trump against Chinese manufacturers, foreign producers in the People's Republic are forced to reschedule. Chief financial officers of Asian firms, which are considering moving their production from China to another country, India in a survey by UBS as one of the preferred alternative.
But now India itself has come into Trump's focus. Derogatorily labeled "Kingdom of Tariffs" by Haudegen in the White House, America deprived the emergen country in early June the trade concessions for 2000 products. The U.S. probably accuses India of high tariffs and calls for better market access for agricultural goods, mobile phones, spirits, motorcycles and more.
On the world map of trade wars, the frictions with India merely represent a sidescay. In addition, America views the world's largest democracy also through the security-political glasses. Delhi and Washington combines the concern about China's claims to power in the South Indian Ocean. Against this background, a salvo of American punitive tariffs against India seems unlikely. India's Prime Minister Narendra Modi, who has just taken a second five-year
The problem, however, lies in another place: The empirically hardened thesis that open markets accelerate the economic development has a difficult position in the socialist republic of India. Even under Modi. Its zeal for reform is aimed mainly at the bureaucratic madness, which suffocated any entrepreneurial spirit in the multi-ethnic state for too long. Companies are now quickly receiving construction permits for buildings. They benefit from a better energy and transport infrastructure
The success of these efforts can be seen and measured in the relevant rankings. From an economic point of visibility, a lot has turned for the better in India in the first five modes. But this does not apply in trade policy. This encourages people to protect non-competitive companies from foreign providers internationally, the national-conservative government even created new barriers.
In the West, Modi depends on the nimbus of the energetic reformer, who radically renews his country economically. This impression is not least due to the fact that the charismatic prime minister knows all too well, which vocabulary Western managers like. In essence, however, the "India First" advocate never moved away from a protectionist economic policy, which prevents Indian companies from being forced into anachronistic processes
Funeral of Jacques Chirac on Monday in Paris
Former French President Jacques Chirac is to be buried in Paris next week, according to a report. The former head of state will be buried on Monday at Montparnasse cemetery in the south of the French capital, AFP news agency reported, citing the Chiracs family. The burial will take place in the close family circle. For Sunday, a public funeral service was scheduled in Invalidendom, according to media reports.
Carried the competitor to the finish: Dabo is the first secret hero of the World Athletics Championships
The top of the world was already long in the finish, when heartwarming scenes took place in the chilved Khalifa International Stadion. In the first run of the men over 5000 meters, Jonathan Busby from Aruba started to sway dangerously. He could hardly stay on his feet a good 300 meters in front of the finish, ran in an unended bent posture, when Braima Suncar Dabo from the West African country
"I just wanted to help him. Everyone in this situation would have done the same thing," Dabo said after the race in Mixed Zone, as he unexpectedly attracted the attention of the assembled journalists. Shyly, he didn't want to talk at all about his feat. After all, he wanted to set up his personal best time and when he realized that this would be difficult, he only wanted to represent a sufferer. And anyway, he would only be proud
That's what he did on the evening - and much more intense than he probably would have thought. Because this feat will be remembered. Especially since Dabo even let his competitor Busby precede the goal and voluntarily took over the last place - by the way, a good five minutes after the Ethiopian Selemon Barega, who won this lead-up. In the end, it was all used, because of that
Dabo, who lives in Portugal and studies there in Lisbon, competed to set a personal best time at the World Championships in Doha. According to official data, this succeeded with 18:10.87 minutes, he himself was nevertheless disappointed: "I wanted to run 17:55 minutes. I've been under 18 minutes". But only in training, best times do not matter.
Multi-million mash: Egyptians gear up for wild day of protest
In Egypt, security measures have been stepped up in the run-up to possible anti-government protests. An increased police presence was observed in central Cairo on Friday. Several subway stations remained closed, roads around central Tahrir Square were closed.
Already in previous days, the police had stepped up their street checkpoints and searched the mobile phones of pedestrians partly on messages and photos in connection with the protests. Hundreds had been arrested, according to two non-government organizations.
Opponent of Al-Sisi
Last weekend there were protests in Cairo, Alexandria, Suez and Mansura with hundreds of participants. They were against the government of President Abdel Fattah al-Sisi, to whom critics accuse corruption and mismanpower. There had not been such an open protest against the political leadership in Egypt for years. The Egyptian contractor living in Spanish exile and actor Mohammed Ali, who started the demonstrations with video messages
According to two observer groups, the number of arrests in connection with the protests continued to rise. The Egyptian Commission for Rights and Freedoms (ECRF) counted more than 1,570 arrests until Friday. The Egyptian Center for Economic and Social Rights (ECESR) as well as the human rights organization Human Rights Watch (HRW) spoke of more than 2,000 arrests. Independently, these figures could not be confirmed at first. The government also did not acknowledge the figures.
Arbitrary Arrest
Many people were "arbitrarily arrested" "just because they were 'in the wrong place at the wrong time,' HRW said, citing those arrested. The accusations against those arrested, according to their lawyers, range from "proliferation of misinformation" to membership in a "terrorist association". In Cairo, Alexandria and other cities, security forces also questioned and searched in uniform, but also in civilian clothing
HRW also criticized censorship in the North African country. According too, the government in Cairo is blocking websites and online services to prevent further protests against Sisi.
Online retailers: Mail-orders should be able to donate returns more easily
For online retailers, many returned parcels are lost goods. Around 20 million returns end up on the garbage. But there is an alternative.
The Ministry of Finance has shown online retailers such as Amazon and Zalando a way they can donate returned products instead of destroying them.
Companies could not be exempted from the sales tax on donations in kind, the ministry said in response to a question from the Green parliamentary group, about which the newspapers of the Funke media group reported on Friday, but the dealers could set the market value of the unsaleable returns so low that they would have to pay no or only little sales tax.
Scientists at the University of Bamberg had determined that German citizens sent back every sixth package for orders on the Internet. Around four percent of the goods - around 20 million products - subsequently ended up in the garbage. Donating them would cost the companies additional money according to the current legal situation, because the tax authorities evaluate donations in kind such as a turnover, to which sales tax is then incurred.
Green parliamentary group leader Katrin Göring-Eckardt urged Finance Minister Olaf Scholz (SPD) to make it clear with a decree that companies cannot evaluate saleable products accordingly and donate them free of charge to non-profit organizations. The companies would have to be able to rely on not being punished for donations. This applies not only to online commerce, but also to other shops from the fashion store to the hardware store.
Banksy art for auction: There has never been a better time to sell this painting
The well-known work of street art star Banksy, "Devolved Parliament," is to be auctioned soon.
For Branczik, Banczy's work underscores "the regression of the world's oldest parliamentary democracy into the basic stance shaped by animals and trunks". From Saturday, the painting is to be exhibited at Sotheby's in the British capital until it is to be auctioned on 3 October. It is valued at 1.5 to two million euros.
The work was first exhibited in 2009 at the Museum of Bristol in south-western Britain, where Banksy lives. This year, the artist had exhibited the painting again - at the same time as the original Brexit date on March 29. On this occasion, the work, formerly called "Question Time," was renamed "Devolved Parliament."
The picture could change hands for an amount of millions.
Most recently, Prime Minister Boris Johnson delivered a hard exchange of blows with the opposition on Wednesday at the first meeting of the House of Commons after a forced break imposed by Johnson by the Supreme Court. Johnson wants to carry out the already twice postponed Brexit on 31 October if necessary even without an agreement with the EU.
It's not the first time that Banksy, whose identity is still unknown, has interfered in the Brexit debate. In Dover in south-eastern England, he painted a picture depicting a mask breaking up a star of the European flag with a chisel. The plant sees thousands of truck drivers and other visitors every daily upon entering the UK.
Brexit debate is like 'walk in park'
The fierce verbal attacks by British Prime Minister Boris Johnson on Brexit opponents in parliament, according to his top adviser, do not contribute to a climate of hatred. Compared to the election campaign before the 2016 Brexit referendum, the current controversy is a "walk in the park," Dominic Cummings said at a book presentation on Friday. "We're enjoying that. We're going (out of the EU)
Johnson had harshly attacked Brexit opponents in parliament in the past few days, accusing them, among others, of "betrayassment" to the people. Accusations that he could put MPs in danger because they could, for example, become the target of death threats, he dismissed as "humbug."
Cummings said the only way to allow calm to return again was if MEPs would finally respect the UK's decision to leave the EU. It was not surprising that people were upset that Britain had still not resigned from the EU three years after the referendum.
In light of the supercharged mood on Friday, even the bishops of the Anglican Church called for restraint. "We should talk to each other with respect," it said in a statement. "And we should also listen."
Pakistan's Prime Minister Khan calls for U.N. intervention in Kashmir
With an emotional appeal, Pakistan's Prime Minister Imran Khan has again called on the United Nations to intervene in the Kashmir conflict during his first appearance before the U.N. General Assembly.
Otherwise, a renewed war between the nuclear powers Pakistan and India is likely, Khan said on Friday at the U.N. general debate in New York. "If this goes badly, you hope for the best, but are prepared for the worst". The United Nations would have a responsibility for Kashmir. "You should have prevented this".
India and Pakistan have fought three wars since their independence from British colonial rule in 1947, two of them around the divided region of Kashmir. Both nuclear powers each dominate part of Kashmir, another part belongs to China. At the beginning of August, India had deprived the Indian Kashmir region of autonomy status and thus abruptly increased tensions in relation to Pakistan.
Not even a bottle of water
Protesters in Cairo are calling for the overthrow of Egyptian President al-Sisi.
Against the current protests in Egypt, the government reacts with a hard hand. It lets the population shoot at the population with live ammunition, over 2000 detainees join the 60,000 political prisoners in the prisons, who are already sitting there. Protesting has never been harmless in Egypt. However, since the seizure of power by General Abdel Fattah el-Sisi in 2013, the state apparatus is doing everything possible
But one of the triggers for the protests in 2011 was the bad economic situation. Poverty, lack of prospects and the feeling of being suffocated by one’s own government drove millions into the streets. And this is a problem, which also the regime of el-Sisi does not get to grips, no matter how hard it takes action against adversaries. The situation has even deteriorated.
Today, one in three people in Egypt lives below the poverty line. Since 2016, the currency has lost more than half of its value, the inflation rate is ten percent. If you want to buy a bottle of water in the supermarket, you have to decide whether you finance: Nestlé, Coca-Cola or the military. The latter is not only responsible for oppression but also the biggest economic actor. The military builds streets, houses, football stadiums
Tüv exams in the terminal: BER boss: No more "showstoppers" for capital airport
For two months it has been checked whether the complicated system braid works in conjunction with BER fire protection. The operators are satisfied with the course. However, the Tüv is not yet finished at BER.
After large-scale Tüv examinations in the terminal of the future capital airport, the operators confirmed the planned opening in October next year. This is safe, said Supervisory Board Chairman Rainer Bretschneider after a meeting of the Supervisory Board.
Airport CEO Engelbert Lütke Daldrup spoke of a successfully completed examination, but also said there were still shortcomings in the terminal to work off. "We have many detailed topics, but we don't have a real showstopper". He assured that the schedule until the commissioning still contains buffers.
However, there are indications that the time reserve should no longer be too large. The construction completion notification is now to be submitted to the construction office in the course of the first quarter 2020, at the beginning of the year it was planned for this autumn.
Hundreds of deficiencies in power cables have yet to be rectified; the Tüv wants to examine them by February. "In order to create the conditions for the start of flight operations, work must continue to be done intensively on the processing of the deficiencies," it said in a written communication.
Lütke Daldrup said that in many cases it is a question of lack of evidence, less about new cables. There is still no approval for all dowels on which the cable routes hang. The airport chief expects these to be available by the end of the year.
In addition, the fire protection at the transition to the station under the terminal does not yet work satisfactorily. The problems had led the project into another crisis three years ago. This time it is about smoke aprons, which shut down in a fire like a canvas, so that the smoke does not spread. They drive down too far. 36 engines must therefore be exchanged. Then there should be new attempts with hot gas smoke at the beginning of the year.
Problems with fire protection and further construction defects as well as planning errors have been delaying the opening of the airport for years. Originally, it was supposed to go into operation in 2011. The costs have risen since construction began in 2006 from two billion to probably around 6.5 billion euros at the opening next year.
Because the number of passengers in Berlin has risen significantly since then, those responsible are planning the expansion of the airport. This is why a special meeting of the Supervisory Board on 18 October, as the Chairman Bretschneider announced.
The airport company is now looking for two new managing directors. Chief Financial Officer Heike Fölster will move to Deutsche Bahn at the end of the year, Human Resources Director Manfred Bobke-von Camen will retire next year, as Bretschneider said. He thanked Fölster. She has put the airport finances on firm footing.
Chemnitz: Vending machines blown up for drugs - police arrest man
He needed money for drugs - and blew up several vending machines in the Chemnitz city area! Now the perpetrator is finally in custody!
In the past two years, the number of blown cigarette and parking ticket vending machines in Chemnitz and the region had risen sharply, resulting in a million-dollar loss!
The police arrested a 33-year-old German after two detonations at the end of May on Bürgerstraße and Further Straße in Chemnitz.
Police spokesman Andrzej Rydzik: "In the course of the investigation because of causing explosive explosions in crime unit with a particularly serious case of the theft, which was conducted by the Chemnitz criminal police, not only confirmed this suspicion against the 33-year-old".
Confronted with the new evidence, the 33-year-old Chemnitzer now confessed to a total of eight deeds.
The police spokesman: "With the loot, he apparently financed his narcotics dependence". Four times alone, the bomber had blown up parking ticket machines on Bürgerstraße between August 20, 2018 and May 31, 2019.
The total damage incurred amounts to at least 60 000 euros!
The vending machine bomber is now in pre-trial detention. The police investigation against him is ongoing.
Conceivable that he was also responsible for further vending vending restrictions in Chemnitz and adjacent counties.
AUA acquires Eurowings flights from Vienna
In the midst of the price war with low-cost airlines such as Lauda, Wizz Air, EasyJet and Level, the Lufthansa subsidiary AUA "takes over the commercial control of all Eurowings flights from Vienna," an Austrian Airlines press spokesman confirmed to the APA on Friday. Previously, "AviationNetOnline" had reported about it.
The Viennese base of the Lufthansa low-cost airline Eurowings will thus be de facto dissolved. Of the currently seven Eurowings aircraft, four will remain in Vienna. From January 2020 onwards, the AUA will take over in the "Wetlease", i.e
The Eurowings base in Vienna will be integrated into the AUA hub and network planning, the spokesperson explained. The flight times are to be matched to connecting flights. On the one hand, the aim is to strengthen the Vienna hub and, on the other hand, to expand decentralized traffic from the federal states.
As AviationNetOnline writes, Eurowings in Vienna only serves as a "production company" for the group's sister AUA. For example, Barcelona and Rome with AUA flight number, but primarily with Eurowings device, would be served. The on-board product should be adapted to that of Austrian Airlines, but the pilots and flight attendants arre from Eurowings. According to the report, the Eurowings flights starting under AUA flight number will no longer take place
A price war broke out at Vienna Airport after the Niki bankruptcy. Ryanair subsidiary Laudamotion only announced on Thursday to increase the fleet in Vienna to 16 aircraft next summer. The Irish low-cost airline sends four Ryanair planes including crews to Vienna, also in the so-called "Wetlease", which the union criticized as a circumvention construct on the collective agreement.
Ryanair CEO Michael O'Leary's battle announcement that Lauda will have overtaken the AUA in five years' time, AUA board member Andreas Otto said on Friday: "We're staying number one at the site, in five, six or ten years."
Queen granddaughter Princess Beatrice is engaged
Beatrice, Eugenie, William and Harry: As children the cousins were best friends. Now marries the last of the four. Beatrice is engaged!
Wonderful news from the British royals: Edoardo Mapelli Mozzi (34) is said to have stopped for the hand of his girlfriend Princess Beatrice (31) during the Italy holidays at the beginning of the month, the palace announced on Thursday.
"We are extremely pleased to announce our engagement. We are both so excited to start this adventure together and can't wait to actually be married," the couple, who have been dating since October 2018, said in a statement.
"We share so many similar interests and values and we know that this will help us in the coming years full of love and happiness."
Sister Eugenie snapped the pictures
The official engagement photos have been taken by her sister, Princess Eugenie (29) - and also immediately posted on her Instagram account.
It's a royal party
The wedding is scheduled to take place in 2020. More information on the date, location and everything else will be announced by the palace in the coming months.
What is certain, however, is that marriaging the number nine of Britain’s succession to the throne and real estate mogul will be as big as Eugenie’s wedding to Jack Brooksbank (33) on 12 October 2018.
Study on Causes of Heart Disease
Smoking, obesity, unhealthy diet - there are risk factors for cardiovascular diseases. But what really triggers heart failure or infarction? Hamburg researchers now want to take a close look at the genome of 9000 healthy and sick people.
Through the analysis of the genomate of 9000 people, hamburgers and Swiss scientists want to discover the causes of cardiovascular diseases in more detail.
The research project is the largest of its kind in German-speaking countries, the Universitätsklinikum Hamburg-Eppendorf (UKE) announced on Friday. The Kühne Foundation is funding the project with 12.5 million euros. From Switzerland, the high mountain clinic Davos, which belongs to the foundation, and the University Hospital Zurich are involved.
Overweight, smoking and unhealthy diets are among the essential affectable risk factors for cardiovascular diseases. Genetic factors determine the risk. However, the interaction between genes and external influences is insufficiently known, it said. Now, the scientists want to compare the complete genetic material of 8000 healthy people with that of 1000 former patients of the university heart and vascular center at the UKE.
Currently, more than 4000 of the approximately 20,000 relevant human genes are aware of possible disease-causing changes in DNA - so-called mutations - according to the hospital. Every year, about 200 would be added.
According to the initiators, the aim of the five-year project is to develop new options such as the prevention, detection and treatment of cardiac arrhythmias, heart failure and heart attacks. The data could also be used in the research of other common diseases, according to Stefan Blankenberg, head of their and vascular center. New findings on the interplay between the heart and brain, the heart and kidney as well as cancer diseases are possible.
Klaus-Michael Kühne (82), chairman of the Kühne-Foundation (82), also emphasized that there was a firm desire to use the data also for the research of other diseases. The clinic of the foundation in Davos so far mainly treats allergies. Now, on the local medical campus, a Center for Cardiovascular Precision Medicine is to be created and a professorship for bioinformatics.
The organic samples of the healthy participants come from hamburgers who also participate in the "Hamburg City Health Study". According to the hospital, this is the largest local health study in the world according to the clinic. In the case of generous decryption, strict attention is paid to the protection of anonymized data, the scientists assured. It is about one petabyte (1000 terabytes) of data, said Holger Moch from the University Hospital Zurich.
According to UKE, a person’s genetic material can be deciphered within 24 hours today. The pure material costs for this would amount to 1300 euros. The first gene sequencing worldwide, the results of which were published in 2001, still cost several hundred million euros. The study is scheduled to start on 1 October, as early as the end of 2020, all 9000 samples are to be analyzed.
Nigerian police free hundreds of abused boys
In Nigeria, police have freed more than 300 tortured and abused boys from a Koran school. According to indications from local residents, security forces searched the school in the northern Nigerian city of Kaduna and discovered the partially still minor victims, police spokesman Yakubu Sabo said. "The victims were mistreated. Some of them claimed to have been raped by their teachers," Sabo said.
The boys had been detained under the pretext of being taught and "improved" in the Koran. In fact, the protégés had been detained by their teachers under "the most degrading and inhumane conditions," Sabo said. About a hundred pupils, including children aged nine, had been chained up in a small room.
The Koran school had existed for about ten years. Residents had alerted the authorities to repeated irregularities in the facility. The parents would have been shocked when they were taught about the conditions in the Koran school, Sabo said. They would have suspected nothing of the sufferings of their children. The operator and six employees were arrested, according to police.
In the predominantly Muslim north of Nigeria, there are many private Koranic schools. They replace the often deficient state institutions. People in the region suffer from frequent attacks by armed gangs. According to the U.N. Refugee Agency, therefore, more than 40,000 people fled across the border to the neighboring state of Niger in the past ten months.
Chained and tortured: Nigeria's police freed 400 children out of school
Police officers have freed nearly 400 pupils from appalling conditions at a school in northern Nigeria. "Many of them were chained up and some had visible injuries pointing to torture," police spokesman Yakubu Sabo said in the city of Kaduna.
There, the children had been discovered on Thursday after a clue in an Islamic school. The conditions there were "unworthy of people". The officials arrested the headmaster and six teachers after the liberation action.
The children, all boys, were held like slaves at their school and put on chains. Some had been sexually abused. Television images showed emaciated and injured students in a recognizably poor state of health.
World footballer's choice: More allegations after Messis and Klopp's freestyle
Football 'signature not valid'
Jurgen Klopp, Lionel Messi and Megan Rapinoe awarded
With a gala in Milan, world football's governing body Fifa honors the best player, the best player and the best coach of the year. For Lionel Messi, it is the sixth honor and thus record.
After Nicaragua's captain declared that he had never voted in the world footballer's election, accusations come to Fifa from other countries. Egypt was not scored at all - with a curious justification.
A lot at Fifa has proven to be infamous in recent years: the World Cup awards, dubious plans of its president Gianni Infantino, the general behavior of various corrupt functionaries. The Fifa world footballer's election, however, has so far not been suspected of being unclean - apart from the fact that the voting national coaches and national team captains
But having already complained that he had not voted at all, further inconsistencies are now emerging: Egypt complains that the votes of national coach Hossam El-Badry and captain Mohamed Salah have not been taken into account. In fact, they do not appear in the official and publicly visible vote overview.
Fifa apologises in that "the signatures on the election forms have been handed in capital letters and have therefore not been valid". They have contacted the Egyptian federation several times to clarify the situation - without reply.
Sudan's coach, too marvelled
There is also an outcry from Sudan regarding the election. While the situation is understandable in the case of Egypt, the Sudanese association states that coach Zdravko Lugarisic did not vote as it was finally scored: with Lionel Messi at number one, Virgil van Dijk on two and Sadio Mané on three.
Accusations had also been made by Nicaragua's captain Juan Barrera: "Didn't vote for the #TheBest2019 Awards. All disclosures about my voice are false, thank you," he wrote. In his case, it was suspected that the Nicaraguan Football Association simply voted on his behalf.
From the association came on Friday now the news: True, but was of course no intention. The completed election documents were "erroneously" provided with the name and the signature of Juan Barrera, the captain of the Nicaraguan selection, it was said in a message of the association. But actually the second captain Manuel Rosas. "We take the responsibility for the administrative error", it was said in the communication
Fifa is now examining the other cases in an investigation, but emphasizes that the election process was supervised externally by the consulting firm PriceWaterhouseCoopers. As long as Lionel Messi remains undisputed winner of the world footballer's choice - and Jürgen Klopp world coach of the year.
Ukraine affair: Senior Republicans speak out in favour of official investigation
Two high-ranking Republicans have openly advocated an official investigation against U.S. president and party friend Donald Trump. "I think the investigation is important," said the Republican governor of the state of Vermont, Phil Scott. "Where that leads from here is determined by the facts that are ascertained." He wants to know more about the whistleblower affair before taking action. An impeachment
Scott said he wasn’t surprised by the revelations. These had shown that Trump had repeatedly urged Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy in a phone call to investigate the activities of Democratic presidential candidate and former Vicinitor Joe Biden in Ukrain. According to Scott, Trump has been observing Biden “for years.”
The Republicans have so far withheld criticism of Trump over the whistleblower affair, only a few have shown unease about it. Scott's remarks are now a first indication of this in Trump's party. In doing so, the moderate governor of Virginia had already objected several times against Trump's policies, such as the easing of environmental regulations and tightening immigration regulations.
As another high-ranking Republican, Charlie Baker, governor of Massachusetts, also spoke out in favor of the investigation. "It's a deeply disturbing situation," Baker said. "The right course of action and responsibility for Congress is now to investigate the matter and get to the bottom of it."
Scott and Baker, as governors of U.S. states, formally have no say in the proceedings against Trump, about which is decided in Congress. Their remarks are therefore above all symbolically important. Nevertheless, first senators of Republicans have also commented critically about the situation. Mitt Romney, for example, who represents the state of Utah in the second parliamentary chamber, said the case is "extremely disturbing, deeply disturbing."
The affair could result in the initiation of impeachment proceedings against Trump, the Democrats in the House of Representatives have announced an investigation. They accuse him of abuse of power for the purpose of foreign influence for his own political benefits.
There is no exact timetable for impeachment proceedings. With their majority in the House of Representatives, Democrats could exert so-called impeachment. However, the decision on an actual impeachment fell in the Senate, where Trump’s Republicans have the majority. The prospects for the success of such proceedings are therefore low. So far, no U.S. president has been removed from office through an impeachment procedure.
Were torturers on the board of Rackete's "Sea Watch"?
Three men have been arrested in Sicily, they are alleged to have raped and murdered in Libyan refugee camps.
The German captain Carola Rackete (31) had been arrested in June after she illegally approached the port of Lampedusa with the rescue ship "Sea Watch 3" and 53 Libyan refugees on board. After violent protests and international criticism - also by Austria's Federal President Alexander der Bellen - her house arrest was revoked after a few days by an Italian judge.
Now new serious accusations against Rackete are becoming loud. Thus, three men who are accused of torture in Libyan refugee camps could have arrived in Italy with the "Sea-Watch 3". The suspects have been arrested at a reception camp in Messina (Sicily) after migrants recognized their tormentors in Italy. "We can't rule that out, but have no secured information on it," said "S
In mid-September, the police said that the suspects in the North African country had raped, tormented and even murdered. It was Mohammed C. (27) from Guinea, and the two Egyptians Hameda A. (26) and Mahmoud A (24). "The migrants were systematically harassed and atrocities suspended - through repeated and constant physical violence," the statement said at the time.
NGO and aid agencies now point out that they can't know exactly who's coming onto the ships. "We can't scan who the people are. They come without passports," Neugebauer said. Torture is one of the main reasons why people are fleeing Libya. It's therefore quite likely that perpetrators are also located on the ships. "It's therefore very important that this is being investigated."
Italy's right-wing ex-interior minister Matteo Salvini immediately demanded an apology from Rackete, who is still understated in Italy for aiding and abetting illegal immigration.
Musk aspires to make delivery record at Tesla
Tesla CEO Elon Musk has given his electric car company a powerful boost in the stock market with the prospect of new record numbers.
"We have the chance to reach our first quarter with 100,000 deliveries," Musk wrote in an e-mail to Tesla employees published by industry blog "Electrek."
Demand is strong, so Tesla could place 110,000 orders, according to Musk’s circular. The challenge is to bring the cars quickly to the clientele. Tesla would have to provide as many resources as possible at the end of the quarter.
Investors liked this: Tesla's shares went out of trading with an increase of over six percent. The price hasn't increased so sharply for almost four months. In the second quarter, Tesla had delivered a good 95,000 cars and thus reached its previous record.
Musk needs records in order to achieve his high goals. In 2019, he wants to deliver a total of 360,000 to 400,000 cars. For this, six-figure figures have to be found in the current and next quarter year. Above all, the cheapest Tesla Model 3 is in demand, but here the profit margins are low. Despite the boom, there have recently been losses.
U.S. budget dispute: Once again just bridge financing
The new financial year will begin in the U.S. on October 1 without a decent budget. The Senate followed the House of Representatives on Thursday and approved transitional funding until November 21.
Although the American Congress and President Donald Trump agreed in August on an increase in spending ceilings for the financial years 2020 and 2021, the United States is once again without spending plans before the new fiscal year beginning on 1 October. The Senate followed the House of Representatives on Thursday and approved a transitional financing (Continuing Resolution, CR) until November 21. Without such a decision, it would have come on Monday at midn
Shutdown averted
For the time being, the federal administration will now be financed at the level and according to the priorities of the spending plans for the financial year 2019, which ends on Monday. This, because Congress has not been able to agree in time on the 12 expenditure laws that allocate funds to the various departments and authorities. For the time being, the federal administration can therefore not benefit from the spending framework expanded from $1.3 to $1.4 for the new financial year.
Democrats have completed tens of twelve bills in the House of Representatives, where they have the majority. However, the spending bills reflect priorities of Democrats who have little chance in the Republican-dominated Senate. Conversely, the Senate also more or less has ten of the twelve spending plans, which are hardly capable of a majority.
Ghosts are differing mainly on the issue of resource allocation for domestic security and border security. In the Senate, Republicans are apparently trying to force $12bn for the construction of the Wall, one of President Trump's big priorities. Democrats are bothered by the wall construction plans and their funding, which is actually earmarked for other military buildings and projects. The dispute over the construction of the Wall had already escalated in December 2018
On the construction of the wall, the spirits differ
Currently, it is unclear how the parties want to get closer on this issue. More likely than an agreement on all twelve spending laws in the coming seven weeks is an extension of transitional financing beyond November 21. Even a shutdown is then not excluded.
Against the background of the ongoing inability of Congress to agree in time on the twelve expenditure laws and the associated risk of a shutdown, ideas to automate the proceedings are gaining popularity. Submission p. 1877 to avoid shutdowns would automatically continue the financing of the federal administration at the previous year’s level, prevent the travel activity of parliamentarians and withhold their wages until the new annual spending plans.
France allows artificial insemination for lesbians and singles
In France, artificial insemination is also supposed to be open to women and lesbians living alone in the future: The Paris National Assembly voted by a large majority in favour of a legislative proposal of the government, according to which all women can take advantage of in-vitro fertilization. This should significantly increase the demand for anonymous sperm donations.
The proposal still requires the approval of the Senate. In the three-day emotional debate, critics in the right-wing camp had warned that numerous "children without a father" could grow up in the future. Also, the French bishop's conference is against the innovation.
The new regulation is the core component of a bioethics law, defending Health Minister Agnès Buzyn as a "chance" for society. It depicts the modern family, in which there are children with only one parent or with homosexual parents. According to her, the costs of artificial inseminations for all women are to be borne by social insurance.
Fertilization with sperma of dead husband still banned
By a narrow majority, the National Assembly, on the other hand, rejected an amendment that was supposed to give widows access to their dead man's cum for artificial insemination. In Belgium, Spain and Britain, artificial insemination "post-mortem" is allowed. In Germany, in the past, there have been cases in which women sued in vain on the release of the sperm of a deceived. The opponents of the advance in Paris
Many MPs of the Ruling La République en Marche (The Republic in Motion, LREM) of President Emmanuel Macron and MPs of the opposition, on the other hand, had advocated artificial insemination with the sperm of the late partner. They emphasized that a widow would be imposed a "double grief" if the death of her husband also left her desire to have children unfulfilled.
The initiators of the now-failed amendment think it is unfair that a widow can be fertilized in the future with the sperm of an unknown person, but not with that of her own partner.
Until now, artificial insemination is only allowed in France to heterosexual couples who cannot father children. They must be married or live together for at least two years. Buzyn had already announced in the summer that surrogacy remains prohibited.
In Germany, artificial insemination is theoretically open to all women - it is often difficult for single or lesbian couples.
Compensatory payment for the climate: Calls for flight tax for universities are getting louder
There is a lot of flights at the universities. That is why the country wants to have a climate levy there in the future. The rectors say that they have no money for it. But now they are also under internal pressure.
Universities are also supposed to restrict themselves when flying. Photograph: dpa/Julian Stratenschulte
The discussion about a climate levy on flights from universities and colleges postponed before the summer break still gets going again. After the state rector conference had reacted skeptically to a corresponding advance from the green-black coalition, the plans now receive support from the universities. In a joint statement, the doctoral students of six Baden-Württemberg universities and an educative college for a compensation of service flights
Russia arrests North Korean fishermen again
According to the FSB intelligence service, the detainees are said to have fished illegally in Russian waters. Ten days earlier, 161 fishermen from North Korea were arrested. At that time, there was an exchange of gunfire between the border guards and the boat crew. Three Russians and a North Koreaner were injured, the fisherman later succumbed to his serious injuries. This time, however, there had been no injuries, it said.
The communist North Korea suffers from chronic food shortages. The North Korean fishermen were therefore traveling in Russian territorial waters, where the coast guard of Russia according to its own information to protect their marine biological resources patrols.
30,000 squid seized
During the renewed checks, 30,000 squid had been spotted on the boats and seized, according to three fishermen's and two motorboats and taken to Nachodka and Posjet near Vladivostok.
Russia's fishing authority had not issued fishing licenses to the North Koreans this year even after lawsuits by local entrepreneurs. Thousands of fishermen from North Korea have already been temporarily arrested there this year, according to the media.
The head of the Association of Russian Fisheries Companies in the Far Eastern region of Primorye, Georgi Martynov, also recently lamented high ecological damage caused by North Korean fishermen. They fished with close-knit synthetic nets banned in Russia. With these, even small creatures would be retrieved from the sea. In addition, the nets would simply be disposed of in the sea, where it would take 200 years.
In the future only 18,000: U.S. lowers asylum ceiling
The "most generous nation in history": Donald Trump, here at a meeting with elected White House police representatives.
The richest country in the world is sealing itself off from those seeking protection: As Foreign Ministry in Washington confirms, the ceiling for the legal influx of refugees will be lowered further. In the future, threatened believers and Iraqis in particular will receive asylum.
The U.S. government wants to lower the already historically low limit for the reception of new refugees again. In the fiscal year 2020, which runs in the United States from October 2019 to September 2020, a maximum of 18,000 people are to be allowed into the country, as the U.S. State Department explained. In the almost expired marketing year, the limit is still 30,000 people.
President Donald Trump's administration has drastically lowered the readiness for protection seekers from all over the world in recent years. Under Barack Obama's predecessor administration, far higher admission limits had applied.
The targeted cap of 18,000 refugees would be the lowest since the introduction of the U.S. refugee program in 1980. The State Department nevertheless described the U.S. as "most merciful and generous nation in history," a formulation strongly reminiscent of the incumbent U.S. president's parlance.
A "responsible treatment" of refugees is aimed at allowing the refugees to return to their home countries in order to help with the reconstruction there, Trump explained. For his hard course in migration policy, he gets a lot of encouragement from supporters, but there is also massive criticism.
A major point of contention is the border with Mexico, which Trump wants to lock down. In order to finance the construction of a border wall, Trump had imposed a national emergency. Whether there is a temporal connection between the current measure and the current domestic political developments in the U.S. capital is unclear. Trump is currently under pressure because of the Zelensky affair and a potentially looming impeachment trial.
The U.S. government wants to focus, according to the Foreign Office, in the future on the reception of two refugee groups: people who are prosecuted because of their faith - and Iraqis who have helped the U.S. military in the country and are thus in danger, according to the State Department's statement. From Honduras, Guatemala and El Salvador, on the other hand, a maximum of 1500 refugees are to be admitted.
The U.S. wants people in particular to apply for asylum from this "northern triangle" of Central America in other countries. Against this background, the United States has only entered into a migration agreement with Guatemala and now with El Salvador and Honduras to curb immigration on the U.S. southern border with Mexico.
Court orders release of orangutan from zoo
An orangutan female has been transferred from the former zoo of Buenos Aires to a reception station for great apes in the USA at the request of the Argentine judiciary. The "Sandra", who was born in 1986 at Rostock Zoo, was granted the rights of a "non-human person", which is why their release was mandatory. The animal was brought to Dallas on Thursday evening by direct flight - but not as a passenger, but
Since the 53-kilogram-heavy monkey is unable to adapt to life in the wilderness, she is to live in the outdoor enclosure of the Center for Great Apes in Florida in the future. There are already 21 other orangutans and 31 chimpanzees, among them "Bubbles", formerly pet of pop star Michael Jackson. Before moving in Florida, however, "Sandra" still has a 40-day quarantine
"Sandra" spent a few years in the former Ruhr zoo in Gelsenkirchen after her birth in Rostock, before she was moved to Buenos Aires in 1995. The "Sandras" fundamental rights process, which was initiated by animal rights activists in 2014, has contributed to the fact that the zoo of Buenos Aires will be converted into an eco-park. By 2023, a research and educational site to preserve biodiversity is to be created there.
Record for Swiss extreme mountaineers: 550 meters in 46 minutes
Extreme mountaineer Dani Arnold has succeeded in a new hussar ride on the almost 3000-meter-high Zinne in the Italian Alps.
Without a net, double bottom or other aids, the Swiss conquered the 550-meter-high north wall of the Great Peak in a new record time.
Dressed in jeans, T-shirt and only supported by absolute dizziness, Arnold needed 46 minutes and 30 seconds for the ascension. With this, he undercuted the old record by almost 19 minutes.
Arnold is now a record holder in Free Solo Speed on four of Europe’s six most important north faces.
Nike benefits from new online marketing strategy
The US sporting goods maker Nike, despite the trade conflict with China, has posted a significant increase in profits. Net profit rose to 1.4 billion dollars (just under 1.3 billion euros) in the past quarter. This is 25 percent more than in the same period of the previous year, as the Group announced on Tuesday. This is mainly due to the new marketing strategy "Nike Direct", with which the Adidas competitor turns directly to its customers online.
Sales rose to 10.7 billion dollars in the three-month period until the end of August. Nike’s sales figures rose most sharply with an increase of 22 percent in China. Thus, the group from Beaverton in the US state of Oregon also benefited from its approach in the People’s Republic to target consumers directly via the Internet or smartphone apps.
Chief Financial Officer Andy Campion announced that Nike will continue to drive the "digital transformation" of the sporting goods manufacturer with strategic investments and thus expand its competitive advantage. Investors were pleased with the quarterly figures - the sporting goods maker's shares rose by more than five percent.
Life-length stadium ban due to racism in Italy
Because of racist comments about football professional Juan Jesus from AS Rome, the club from the Italian capital has imposed a lifetime stadium ban against a fan. Defender Jesus, who is from Brazil, had been insulted by the fan on Instagram. Jesus then published the private messages and urged his club to take action. "@officialasroma you already know what to do with such a fan.
The club reacted late on Thursday. He had reported the fan's account on Instagram and the police. In addition, the Roma supporter should never again attend a game of the club, the club wrote on Twitter.
Racism is a problem in Italian football. Regularly, Italian fans chant racist insultations against black players from rival teams. "We can't have racism in society or in the sport. In Italy, the situation hasn't improved, and that's very serious. You have to identify those responsible and throw them out of the stadiums," Fifa president Gianni Infantino had said at the weekend.
But there had also recently been discussions about the supporters of Juventus Turin. The Italian authorities have now imposed stadium bans on 38 rioting fans of record champion Juventus Turin. Four of them are unlikely to enter the stadium for a period of ten years, the police said. For the rest, therefore, bans from four to seven years apply.
The fans are linked to ongoing investigations against twelve leaders of ultra-fan groups around Juventus. The twelve are accused of, among other things, serious extortion, money laundering and private violence. The club had started the investigation with an advertisement last year.
According to Italian media reports, the ultra-leaders are said to be behind the black trade with tickets. They would have "militarily controlled" the ticket trade for the fan curve and are said to have extorted Juventus employees to get cheaper tickets.
Generation Z: Are they really different from Generation Y?
Millennials are narcissistic, Generation Z is political - entire vintages are covered with gripping designations. If you take a closer look, the awards are full of contradictions. So how well do these labels describe the respective attitude to young people?
"The crabs are coming!" With this call officially launched the crab catcher at Gucheng Lake in Gaochun on September 21, which also ushered in the opening event of the 19th China Gaochun Gucheng Lake Crab Festival, as announced by the Nanjing-Gaochun District Committee's Public Relations Department.
Gaochun, which forms the southern foothills of Nanjing in Jiangsu province, is one of the national environmental zones. The city is the first international "Slow City" in China and here is also the Chinese headquarters of the international "Slow City" movement. The city has ideal conditions in ecological terms with a mixture of "30 percent mountains, 20 percent water areas and 50 percent fields".
The people of Gaochun have already started crabbing in the early 1990s. Over time, the crab industry has developed into a formative industry that ensured high incomes and became an important part of Gaochun’s economic development. In recent years, Gaochun has been actively seeking a large-scale, ecological and scientific development of crab farming, as well as a technology-backed crab.
In recent years, Gaochun has brought forward a reform to "distribute power, optimization of administration and government services," and carefully built the service network for "approvals under one roof, a team for law enforcement, a network for services, a platform for lending." With this, the city wants to comprehensively optimize the environment for approvals, for services and for development.
Gaochun fully exploits the on-site advantages through the connection to the southern Jiangsu and the southern Anhui and uses the development zone and its position as an international "Slow City" as figureheads to promote intelligent high-end producing companies, companies for biomedial and medical technology, for energy storage and environmental protection, from the cultural and health industry as well as companies for ecological agriculture, which are a leader in the "3+2" area
Iran-U.S. conflict: Rouhani speaks of end of sanctions - Trump disagrees
Iranian President Hassan Rouhani, after his return from the U.N. General Assembly, spoke of an alleged U.S. offer to lift sanctions. The United States, in return for talks with the Tehran leadership, had promised an end to all sanctions, Rouhani explained on its official website.
U.S. President-elect Donald Trump, however, contradicted the Iranian counterpart on Twitter: "Iran wanted me to lift the sanctions imposed on him so that there would be a meeting. I said, of course, no."
Ruhani describes what happened as follows: German Chancellor Angela Merkel, British Prime Minister Boris Johnson and French President Emmanuel Macron had been present in New York and insisted that a meeting take place. "And America says I lift the sanctions," Rouhani added. The nature of the punitive measures, which should be terminated, has been up for debate
However, Rouhani criticized that this push had not taken place in an acceptable way. In view of the punitive measures in force and "the poisoned atmosphere of maximum pressure," no one could predict the outcome of negotiations.
Earlier in the week, Chancellor Angela Merkel had met with Rouhani in New York. The background was the Europeans’ hopes to save the nuclear deal negotiated with Russia and China with Iran. This is intended to prevent the Islamic Republic from developing nuclear weapons. France’s President Emmanuel Macron, like Merkel, had expressed the hope on the sidelines of the U.N
Severe earthquake near Istanbul - Nearly 200 aftershocks
After western Turkey was shaken by two earthquakes within a very short time, fears of a disaster are growing. 34 people were injured in Tuesday's earthquake. Since the quake the previous day, there have been nearly 200 aftershocks, Turkish Vice President Fuat Oktay said on Friday. With this, there is growing concern of a disaster.
14 schools in Istanbul were closed for security reasons. Hundreds of buildings were damaged. The 5.8 magnitude earthquake had shaken the Marmara region off Istanbul on Thursday and sparked panic in the metropolis of millions. Now the government wants to use a commission to better prepare the country for earthquakes in the future, Oktay announced.
In the past 20 years, Turkey has not been sufficiently prepared for quakes, Istanbul Mayor Ekrem Imamoglu said. In the city itself, the latent danger is repeatedly ignored. Every year, ill-constructed apartment buildings collapse - among other things, weary made by the many smaller earthquakes that regularly shake Istanbul.
City planners such as the well-known architect Mücella Yapici have been warning for years that large open areas, which have been registered in the center as refuges, have long since been built. It was not until the 20th anniversary of the Gölcük quake in August that the Istanbul Civil Engineering Chamber had warned that an estimated one million buildings in Istanbul were not earthquake-proof.
Thursday's epicenter of Thursday's quake lay in the Marmara Sea in front of Silivri district and at a depth of around seven kilometers, according to Afad's Civil Protection Agency. A smaller aftershock caused even greater panic on the roads a few minutes later.
Istanbul mayor Ekrem Imamoglu shared on Twitter that injuries or damage had not been reported at first. The state broadcaster TRT showed people running out of homes in panic. Pupils were taken out of the buildings in Silivri district for security reasons. Some pupils were crying, as can be seen in pictures.
Istanbul is considered to be particularly endangered by earthquakes. Under the Marmara Sea lies a section of the so-called North Anatolian disturbance, which marks the border between the Eurasian and Anatolian slabs. Turkey is repeatedly shaken by earthquakes. One of the deadliest was the quake near Istanbul in 1999. At that time, more than 17,000 people died.
Researchers fear that a 7.1 to 7.4 magnitude earthquake could threaten. But it is unclear when the earthquake occurs. In the earthquake on Thursday, it hit the west of the country and Istanbul again.
Companies design contingency plans, private individuals have so-called go-bags packed with everything you necessary standing next to front doors, and schools teach children how to behave when the earth wobbles. When the next big quake will come is unclear.
Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan, after his return from New York, said eight people had been injured. Some buildings had suffered slight damage. Istanbul Mayor Ekrem Imamoglu tracked developments from Istanbul's Disaster Coordination Center (Akom). According to him, two minarettes of mosques collapsed in Avcilar and Sariyer districts.
Warning for aftershocks in Istanbul
On the social networks, many users report panic. Buildings wobbled. Schools and hospitals were evacuated. Phone lines were interrupted. It had been more violent than that of Tuesday, writes one user. The boss of Turkey's Red Crescent, Kerem clinic, warned via Twitter of aftershocks. He called on people to stay away from buildings that had suffered visible damage.
The timing of the quake, according to official figures, is stated at 13.59 p.m. (local time). Just a few minutes after the first quake, according to CNN Turkey, there was an aftershock indicated with a magnitude of 4.0 on the Richter scale.
Only on Tuesday, an earthquake of magnitude 4.6 had shaken the Turkish Marmara region - it was felt until Istanbul. Its epicenter was also located in the Marmara Sea west of the metropolis of millions, the civil protection agency Afad said on Tuesday.
It wasn't the only earthquake that made headlines this Tuesday: Pakistan, too, was hit by a major earthquake.
Scientists assume that sooner or later there could be a catastrophic earthquake in Istanbul - the tension between the tectonic plates is extremely high. Scientists believe that at some point it unloads itself - and will cause massive damage.
Earthquake scientist Sükrü Ersoy reckons that the earthquake is now a preliminary warning. It has become more likely, he told CNN Türk. "These could be the signs of a major earthquake," Ersoy told the station. "Sooner or later, the big quake will also come."
A few weeks ago there had already been an incident - the quake sparked great worries also in Istanbul. In Albania, there had also been a violent earthquake only a few days ago - there were 68 injured. Also on Lake Constance this year the earth had shaken.
Parliament: New Igniter in Ukraine affair
Two explosive documents provide for new fuel in the Ukraine affair over possible abuse of power by US President Donald Trump. On the way to a possible impeachment, the US Democrats are strengthened: The complaint filed by an anonymous whistleblower about Trump and his controversial phone call with the Ukrainian president harbors important indications for investigations against the president. Trump himself once again sees himself as the victim of a "wit
U.S.: United Airlines passenger jailed in toilet - flier diverted
Because a female passenger was locked in the board toilet, a flight has been diverted by United Airlines. The toilet door had been blocked during flight 1554 from Washington to San Francisco, the airline said, according to U.S. media.
At an unscheduled stopover on Wednesday evening (local time) in Denver, the woman could finally be freed from the narrow cabin. About an hour she had been imprisoned in it, U.S. media wrote, citing other passengers. For onward flight in the direction of San Francisco, passengers had to change planes.
In a video circulated on the Internet, according to U.S. mediaries from Flight 1554, it can be seen how several people try to free the woman. "We're working to open the door at the moment. We'll get you out soon, okay?" says a man. The airline apologized to all passengers. It was initially open whether the woman receives compensation.
The cockroach: Inspired by Kafka: Ian McEwan writes Brexit novel
How does a writer like Ian McEwan deal with all the Brexit chaos? He writes a book about it - and his protagonist is unmistakable.
Inspired by Franz Kafka's famous narrative "The Transformation," British successful author Ian McEwan (71) has written a book about Brexit chaos in Britain.
The novel "Die Kakerlake" (Original: "The Cockroach") will be published in German on 27 November, Diogene's publishing house (Zurich) announced. It is about the transformation of a man named "Jim Sams", who was ignored or hated in his previous life, but is now suddenly Britain's most powerful man.
McEwan was quoted as saying: "When parliament is closed, so that the government cannot be challenged at a critical moment, if ministers shamelessly lie like the Soviet leaders, if Brexiteers, in high positions, downright the disaster of a no-deal, then a writer has to ask himself what he can do. There's only one answer to it: writing."
Kafka's 1915-published narrative is about Gregor Samsa, who wakes up one morning and sees himself "transformed into a tremendous vermin", is gradually excluded from family and society and eventually perishes. "Ian McEwan bows before Kafka to describe a world that stands head," Diogene's publishing house said.
EU invests 9.5 million euros in Austria's railway infrastructure
11,200 Wagons in Austria and Hungary receive whisper brakes in order to reduce the noise pollution caused by freight trains. In addition, sections of the railway connections between Graz and Györ and Wiener Neustadt and Sopron will be modernized and electrified.
This, according to the EU authority, is intended to make travel more comfortable and reduce CO2 emissions. The funds come from the "Connecting Europe Facility".
Thomas Cook: All trips cancelled until 31 October
The German tour operator Thomas Cook has canceled all trips up to and including October 31, 2019 a day after his bankruptcy application. Travel could "not be started for insolvency law reasons," the company announced on Thursday evening.
This also affects bookings of the Thomas Cook Signature, Thomas Cook Signature Finest Selection, Neckermann Reisen, ÖGER TOURS, Bucher Reisen and Air Marin, which have already been paided or paid in full. As is the case for trips starting from 1 November 2019, the company left open. In coordination with insolvency insurance, the further procedure will be examined, it said.
Thomas Cook in Germany had come into the wake of the bankruptcy of the British parent company and filed for bankruptcy on Wednesday. The company, which includes Neckermann Reisen, Öger Tours and Bucher Reisen, employs about 2000 people in Germany.
Insurer wants to reburse hotels from now on
On the other hand, from this Friday, hotels abroad, which currently still host Thomas Cook holidaymakers, should receive money from Zurich insurance. The payments to the hotels are, according to Zurich, attached to the condition that holidaymakers are no longer asked for extra payments.
On the basis of the booking information and without presentation of separate invoices, the hotels in the holiday resorts should now receive 50 percent of the outstanding payments, as a Zurich spokesperson of the German Press Agency said. "This applies immediately and under the condition that Thomas-Cook travelers from these hotels will not now be asked or otherwise coerced for the individual payment of hotel invoices," spokesman Bernd Engelien further said.
The insurance thus reacted to reports from hotel guests who, according to their own information, had been forced to pay for their local accommodation themselves. Some therefore had to put more than 1000 euros on the table. The German Travel Association (DRV) and the Federal Association of Consumer Headquarters had sharply criticized this.
How many hotels are affected will be coordinated on Friday with the insolvent travel provider, the insurance spokesman said. According to data from Thursday, 70 000 Thomas-Cook flat-rate holidaymakers from Germany were still on the way. Zurich Germany has secured holidays and the return journey of Thomas Cook customers.
Insolvency administrators want to try to continue the three Thomas Cook companies in Germany and find a future solution.
However, the now announced withdrawals to hotels by Zurich Insurance only help the travelers who are currently still on the road. For Thomas Cook customers, who still want to fly on vacation, it looks bad. Late on Thursday evening, the company announced that all trips up to and including 31 October would be cancelled. This also applies to such bookings, which have already been paid.
As is the case with start from 1 November 2019, the company left open. In coordination with the insolvency insurance, the further procedure will be examined, it said. In principle, Zurich insurance is also responsible for these cases. The sale of new trips has been stopped. dpa
The company refers to insurer Zurich, which is represented by the company Kaera. Customers can inform themselves on the Kaera website about the possibilities of refunding the travel price and assert their claims via a web form.
Price Harry destroys explosive mine in Angola
For a long time, Prince Harry has been carrying out the commitment of Lady Diana. In Africa, he is now walking directly in the footsteps of his mother.
Dressed with a blue splinter vest, Britain's Prince Harry has detonated and destroyed an explosive mine in Angola.
Near a camp of the British Halo Trust at the town of Dirico, he produced the ignition circle in front of running TV cameras on Friday morning. At a safe distance, the explosive device exploded in the savannah landscape without causing harm. Although the Halo Trust alone destroyed more than 100,000 relics of the decades-old bloody conflict in the African country, there are still more than 1000 minefields that threaten the population.
On the fifth day of his trip to Africa, Harry wanted to follow directly in the footsteps of his mother Diana (1961-1997) in the city of Huambo. With the same explosive master who once accompanied his mother, he wanted to put back the path she walked at the time. The pictures on which Princess Diana walked through a vacated minefield in 1997 had gone around the world. She, too, had demonstrated support for the Halo Trust.
Harry (35) and his wife Meghan (38) have been embarking on a ten-day trip to Africa that has begun in the South African tourist metropolis of Cape Town since Monday.
Stalled Brexit negotiations: The danger of a no-deal remains acute
Actually, Prime Minister Boris Johnson had clearly decided: With him, there is guaranteed to be a third postponement of the Brexit negotiations. But which statements in the polarizing Briton are already of perpetual duration. The recent judgment of the Supreme Court in the UK floats like a Damocles sword over the blonde. His suspension of the parliament, in order to be able to carry out his Brexit course until the withdrawal date, Johnson was cancelled earlier this week
Boris Johnson thinks deal is still possible
But the pressure does not seem noticeable either. Meanwhile, when visiting a hospital, he hinted that in the event of a non-agreement until the end of October, he supported an extension of the negotiations:
You know, we'll abide by the law.
At the same time, however, we are confident that we will leave the EU on 31 October.
And best with a deal. I'm cautiously optimistic that'll work.
And the time is gradually running out for Brexit negotiators in Brussels. The EU and the British negotiators still have about six weeks time to prevent an exit of Britain from the European Union without agreements. However, even the recent talks have not achieved a breakthrough again. A deal until the end of October currently seems rather difficult to imagine. If the British Prime Minister does not break the law, a renewed postponement of the withdrawal date is somehow in effect.
U.S. agency: More e-cigarette deaths
The number of deaths related to e-cigarette use in the U.S. has risen to twelve. In addition, there are 805 confirmed and probable injuries in this context. According to a study in the "New England Journal of Medicine", there have already been more than 900 people, around 500 of them confirmed. The cause of lung damage is still completely unclear. In Europe, there is no similar increase so far
Fears of new protests in Egypt are growing
The videos of Mohammed Ali have been shared in online networks millions of times. The Spanish-based contractor and actor is calling for a "people's revolution". For today Friday, he has called for further protests against President Abdel Fattah al-Sisi and the Egyptian military, whom critics accuse of corruption and mismanagement. Ali is hoping for mass rallies with one million participants in central places in the country.
In any case, the security authorities are alarmed. An increased police presence has already been observed in the centre of Cairo. Several subway stations remained closed, roads around the central Tahrir Square have been closed. Already in recent days, the police had stepped up their street checkpoints and searched the mobile phones of pedestrians partly on messages and photos in connection with the protests.
On September 20, protesters also gathered around Tahrir Square.
Last weekend there were protests in Cairo, Alexandria, Suez and Mansura with hundreds of participants. There had not been such an open protest in Egypt for years. Correspondingly, the security forces reacted harshly. According to the human rights organization Human Rights Watch (HRW), almost 2000 people have been arrested since last week. Many of them have been arbitrarily arrested - only because they were "in the wrong place"
The organization also criticized censorship in the North African land. According to this, the government in Cairo is blocking websites and online services to prevent further protests against al-Sisi. The general has ruled the country since 2013, having overthrown democratically elected Islamist presidentialist Mohammed Morsi. Thousands of Islamists, government opponents, bloggers and activists have been imprisoned and partially convicted in recent years.
"Ocean Change": Arved fox of Greenland expedition back
Polar explorer Arved Fuch has returned from his Greenland expedition on Friday. He was greeted on his arrival at the Port of Hamburg, among others by the skipper Boris Herrmann, who had brought climate activist Greta Thunberg with the racing yacht "Malizia" across the Atlantic.
With a team of scientists, Fuchs had investigated and documented the consequences of global warming in recent months on his "Dagmar Aaen" sailing ship on the coasts of Iceland and Greenland.
The Fridays for Future movement is a blessing, the polar explorer said. He had waited for years for the young generation to speak out. For decades, it has been known that the Earth heats up. Nevertheless, many acted as if there were a planet B.
"What happens on the sea often takes place to the exclusion of the public," Fuchs said. He and his team wanted to inform people about the effects of global warming and the littering of the seas. During the expedition, among other things, Greenlandic glacial ice had been measured with a drone and fished with nets for microplastics, it said.
For his "fight for a sustainable and respectful use of the world's oceans", the polar explorer from Bad Bramstedt was awarded the "Seadevcon Maritime Award" as part of the Hamburg Climate Week. "For all of us who care about the survival of the oceans, Arved Fuchs is a great role model," said skipper Boris Herrmann at the award ceremony
Expensive spaghetti in Rome: Tourists paid 430 euros for noodles
430 euros for two plates of spaghetti with fish in a simple restaurant near the Engelsburg in Rome: This seemed too much for two Japanese tourists. The bill with 80 euros tip included was paid by the two women, but they posted the receipt on Facebook, which triggered heated reactions.
"Such cases harm the reputation of Rome. We urge the police to investigate the question of whether it is a fraud here," demanded a spokesperson from Federagit, the Association of Guides in Rome, according to the Roman daily Il Messaggero (Friday's edition).
Promptly came the reaction of the owners of the restaurant. The Japanese had ordered not only spaghetti, but also fresh fish, the local owner justified himself. However, this is not apparent from the bill. A tip of up to 20 percent of the bill is also normal, the innkeeper said.
U.S. President-elect Donald Trump could actually face impeachment after the tumultuous revelations of the past few days. The Democrats now want to examine this as soon as possible with their investigation in the House of Representatives - there is talk of weeks until a decision would be made.
At the beginning of the scandal is supposedly the phone call with Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky. The accusation of the whistleblower, who ultimately brought the conversation out of concern for national security and democracy of the U.S. with his complaint to the view, is monstrous: The president has abused the power of his office in order to exploit the government of another country for an influence on the 2020 election.
The core of this project can also be traced in the now-published transcript of the conversation with Zelenskyj, although she does not reproduce the full wording. However, the phone call of July 25 is only an episode of a much larger story, in the center of which, in addition to Trump, is above all his personal lawyer: Rudy Giuliani.
From the point of view of the public in May 2019, the story begins when the New York Times of Rudy Giuliani's travel plans reports to Ukraine. He wants to meet in Kiev with the just-elected President Zelensky, and the lawyer himself knows very well that his intentions are delicate. "We don't interfere in an election, we get involved in an investigation, for which we have the right"
The lawyer and former New York mayor appears to have become infected with right-wing conspiracy fantasies fixated on Trump’s opponent in the 2016 election: Hillary Clinton has conspired with actors in Ukraine, critics said at the time to construct an occasion for the Russia investigation in the U.S
It is about exactly the investigations that Trump will talk about on the phone with Zelenskyy so prominently in July. The version Giulianis and the president is: The former vice-president and currently promising candidate for the presidential candidacy of the Democrats, Joe Biden, as Ukraine commissioner of the Obama administration on the dismissal of the then Ukrainian Attorney General Viktor Shokin.
In fact, the procedure was slightly different. When Joe Biden demanded that the Ukrainian Prosecutor-General leave, the investigation against the gas company had already been completed, Hunter Biden was never directly affected. Shokin himself was also accused of deeply corrupt behavior, the U.S. government was by far not the only Western partner pushing for his dismissal. Incidentally, the Obama administration even explicitly supported the investigation against the company.
Disturbances on Instagram Facebook and Whatsapp: Social media is down
Users worldwide report massive disruptions on Instagram and Facebook. According to Germany, Great Britain and Spain are particularly affected. On the website allestörungen.de, users report that Facebook has not loaded since about 15.30 p.m. Again and again. Instagram also issues the error message that content could not be uploaded. Whatsapp also seems affected by the disruption, albeit to the same extent so far.
Instagram and Co. more often affected by disturbances
It’s not the first massive incident this year. As early as March and July, the services had dropped out for several hours. And, as in previous cases, users are also resorting to Twitter this time to air their annoyance.
Polish scientists rescued in Antarctica
Three Polish scientists have been rescued by the Chilean Air Force from the ice of Antarctica. They were traveling with an inflatable boat and several miles from their base when their engine failed. They were lucky in the misfortune - less than an hour it took the Chilean rescue helicopter for salvage.
Eduardo Mosquera, commander of Chilean's 4th Air Brigade:
"At 9:30 a.m. we received the news, at just before ten we started, and a good 20 minutes spàter we were over the inflatable".
Poland has been operating Henryk Arctowski Polish Antarctic Station on King George Island since 1977 , with 19 scientists working there. The frozen researchers have been safely brought back to their research station.
Attention: NIE wireless charging
Conveniently, but harmful
Inductive tensure via Qi standard is extremely practical, but should not be used.
Since several new smartphones have recently come on the market that support wireless charging and sell very well - especially the new iPhone 11 models, we would like to recall a study from the beginning of July. The wireless charging of mobile devices is extremely practical, but not necessarily recommended.
Higher Temperature Adds Battery
Researchers warn against the wireless charging of smartphones. According to the findings of the University of Warwick, this is extremely harmful for the devices. The reason for this is the significantly greater heat generation. If a mobile phone is normally recharged by cable, the battery heats up within three hours to 27 degrees. During wireless charging, however, even with the perfect orientation of the smartphone on the charging pad, the mobile phone always rises above 30 degrees.
Capacitance and lifetime
According to the researchers, the temperature for the durability of batteries is a decisive factor. Every single degree plays a role. When charging with higher temperatures, the battery not only repents in capacity, but it does not last so long overall. The service life is therefore lower. The researchers therefore advise to always charge the smartphone via cable. Since battery replacement for modern smartphones is always associated with high cos, this should be a helpful tip.
Convenient
The technology is extremely convenient: Just put the device on a corresponding charging pad - these are already installed in many restaurants, airports, hotels and cars - and the battery is charged without annoying cable salad and arduous plug fumbling. Since Qi (stands for inductive energy transmission) a worldwide standard is enforced, in the case, according to the researchers, there are hardly any restrictions
Everest traffic jam photographer: 13 eight-thousanders in six-month
According to a Nepalese mountaineer, 13 of the world’s 14 highest mountains have climbed within just six months. Nirmal Purja (36) has reached the top of Manaslu in Nepal and thus set a record, his expedition organizer said.
The former soldier wants to climb all 14 eight-thousanders in seven months. Purja calls his projection "Mission Possible". He has made international headlines before: From him comes a photo of mountaineers who went viral in the spring, who queue on Mount Everest.
The image of the mass rush sparked a debate about stronger safety measures on Earth's 8848-metre-high mountain and highest peak. In May, eleven climbers had died during the ascent within a few days.
One problem for Purja could be the ascent of the 14th eight-thousander: Chinese authorities are currently blocking the Xixabangma mountain in Tibet. The reason for this is bad weather, among other things. At the beginning of week, Purja wrote on Twitter that Nepal’s government wants to talk to China in order to allow him the rise.
Four dead elephants discovered in Sri Lanka
Among the dead animals is also a pregnant elephant cow, a police spokesman said on Friday. "We suspect that the elephants were poisoned".
The four carcasses were found in a sanctuary near the famous and frequented by many tourist former rock fortress Sigiriya. The animals were presumably between 15 and 25 years old, according to the police spokesperson. An autopsy was ordered to determine the cause of death.
In Sri Lanka, almost 200 elephants are killed every year. The perpetrators are often farmers on whose land the animals had previously kept themselves harmless. At the same time, around 50 people are killed by elephants every year. This usually happens when the animals move through villages built near their habitat.
Only about 7,000 elephants are estimated to live in Sri Lanka and their killing is theoretically subject to the death penalty.
Detained British oil tanker leaves Iran
The British oil tanker "Stena Impero" is leaving the port city of Bandar Abbas more than two months after it was arrested by Iran. This was announced by the seafaring office Bandar Abbas, according to IRNA news agency. The Iranian Revolutionary Guards had detained the British-flagged tanker on July 19 under the accusation of having disregarded regulations of maritime law in the Persian Gulf.
Management of Thomas Cook criticized
The competent economic committee of the lower house wants to comprehensively investigate the bankruptcy of the travel giant Thomas Cook. It should be about the salary packages of the board as well as the question of why the British government did not want to financially join the surviving company. "Our interest applies to the supervision in the company as well as the control by external auditors," informed the committee chairwoman Rachel Reeves in London
According to experts, genes are overestimated in colorectal cancer risk
The role of genes in familially increased colorectal cancer risk has apparently been overestimated so far. Researchers from the German Cancer Research Center (DKFZ) Heidelberg come to this conclusion in a recent study. Other risk factors such as familial dietary habits are therefore probably more severe than previously assumed. This has consequences for calculations of the individual risk of colorectal cancer, the DKFZ scientists emphasize.
Elon Musk lets Tesla shares rise
Musk wrote this in an e-mail to Tesla employees published on Thursday (local time) by the industry blog "Electrek". The demand is strong, so that Tesla could place 110'000 orders, according to Musk's Circular.
The challenge is to bring the cars quickly to the customers. Tesla would have to spend as many resources as possible at the end of the quarter.
Investors liked the information: Tesla's shares went out of trading with an increase of over six per centers. The price has not increased so sharply for almost four months. In the second quarter, Tesla had delivered a good 95'000 cars and thus reached its previous record.
Musk needs records to achieve his high goals. In 2019, he wants to deliver a total of 360'000 to 400'000 cars. For this, six-figure numbers have to be found in the current and next quarter-year. Above all, the cheapest Tesla Model 3 is in demand, but here the profit margins are low. Despite the boom, there have been losses recently.
Wants to circumvent British government Brexit law? Johnson reluctant
British Prime Minister Boris Johnson is concerned about whether he is looking for possibilities of circumventing the law against an unregulated Brexit recently passed by parliament. In response to a corresponding question, he told reporters on Friday that his government will abide by applicable law.
The law stipulates that the government must request a postponement of the withdrawal date if no agreement is reached with the EU by mid-October. Johnson criticizes this as a "capitulation law" that makes the negotiations with the EU more difficult.
Former British Prime Minister John Major on Thursday suspected that Johnson could try to use "political harassment" to circumvent the law. Johnson could use a procedural trick that postpones the law's entry into force until after EU exit.
Johnson said he's aiming for a contract with the European Union. "We're confident we'll leave the EU on October 31, and the best way is the one with an agreement."
Iran apparently ramps up uranium enrichment
Irania's President Hassan Rouhani has increased the pressure on European contractors in the dispute over rescuing the historic nuclear deal. "Unfortunately, European states show ineptitude or unwillingness when it comes to concrete action," Rouhani said Thursday at a news conference on the sidelines of the U.N
The background is the conflict over the international agreement to prevent an Iranian nuclear bomb, which in return contained the commitment to Tehran to normalize economic relations. The U.S. single-handedly stepped out of the agreement in 2018 to force Iran with "maximum pressure" to a change of course in the Middle East policy deemed aggressive. The government of President Donald Trump wants to negotiate a new agreement with tougher conditions and is now relying on hard sanctions against Tehran
The International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA) confirmed in a report that the Islamic Republic now also uses the centrifuges installed at the beginning of September with uranium or at least has prepared them for use. According to IAEA data, Iran is also preparing the construction of more than 300 other centrifuges.
Perhaps the Europeans are unwilling to bear their part of the deal and therefore left the Iranians alone with the costs and the burden of oppressive U.S
Rouhani continues to deny Iran's involvement in attacks in Saudia
However, after an attack on an important oil plant in Saudi Arabia in mid-September, Germany, France and the United Kingdom followed the U.S. assessment on Monday that Tehran was responsible for the attack. Originally, the Iranian-backed Houthi rebels in Yemen had confessed to the attack, but this was seen by the U.S
"Those who make such accusations have to provide evidence of this," Rouhani said. However, European leaders would not have been able to do this in the talks with him during the U.N. general debate. Instead, they would only have invoked that the rebels in Yemen could not carry out an attack like this. Rouhani had met in the past few days with German Chancellor Angela Merkel, France's President Emmanuel Macron and British Prime Minister Boris Johnson.
Before possible talks with U.S. President Trump, the far-reaching sanctions of the U.S. administration would first have to be lifted, Rouhani demanded. He urged the government in Washington to pivot to a policy of "dialogue and sanity."
Comet of other star thwarts solar system
Astronomers are currently observing a comet from the depths of the universe. This was achieved first. The comet allows conclusions to be drawn about other solar systems.
What was initially just a presumption is now confirmed: an interstellar guest cruises through our solar system. The comet from the depths of space was baptized on 2I/Borisov after its discoverer and aroused the interest of astronomers around the world.
The well-traveled comet had been spotted on August 30 by amateur astronomer Gennadi Borissov at Margo Observatory in Crimea - with a self-built telescope of 65 centimeters in diameter. Observations of various observatories not only confirmed that it is an unknown comet, but also showed that it did not come from our solar system.
2I/Borisov is the second interstellar object that was sighted when our solar system flied out after 1I/'Oumuamua in 2017.
2I/Borisov offers astronomers the rare chance to get insights into a foreign solar system from nearby before disappearing again. Initial observations show that the interstellar comet resembles those of our own system, as reported by the astrophysical institute of the Canary Islands. "The current speed of the comet is high, about 150,000 kilometers per hour, which at this read
According to the analyses, the comet travels on a so-called hyperbolic railway, which takes it out of our system - and unambiguously of interstellar origin, as announced by the International Astronomical Union (IAU). It is also responsible for the naming and categorization of celestial bodies. In honor of its explorer, the interstellar tail star, which had initially been listed under the catalogue number C/2019 Q4
The astronomers are 1I/'Oumuamua lucky: In contrast to 1I/'Oumuamua, 2I/Borisov has already been sighted. Thus, the researchers will have many months for investigations. "The object will reach the greatest brightness in mid-December and then be visible with medium-sized telescopes until April 2020," Farnocchia explained. "After that, it will only be observed with larger, professional telescopes until October 2020."
Several observatories have already targeted the visitor. For 1I/''Oumuamua, a team led by Karen Meech of the University of Hawaii, who had already led the characterization of Oumuamua, determined the diameter of the celestial object at 2 to 16 kilometers in an initial investigation. Shots by the Gemini observatory in Hawaii show a clear tail of the comet.
Researchers at the Astrophysical Institute of the Canaries (IAC) have already gained a first so-called spectrum from the interstellar tail star, which offers a kind of fingerprint of its chemical composition. "The spectrum of this object resembles those of comets of our solar system, and this indicates that their composition must be similar," reported IAC researcher Julia de Leó
According to the IAU, the discovery of two interstellar visitors within just two years indicates that these objects could open up a new path to the exploration of certain processes in other solar systems. However, it is still difficult to estimate how frequent such visits are. One of the next interstellar visitors could possibly intercept a planned satellite mission from the European Space Agency Esa.
The agency intends to station the three-part spacecraft "Comet Interceptor" by 2028 on a waiting position 1.5 million kilometers away from Earth. According to Esa science director Günther Hasinger, it should either lurk on a still untouched, fresh comet from our own solar system 1I/'Oumuamua, or an interstellar visitor.
First landing on Beijing's new mega-airport Daxing
Just in time before the 70th birthday of the People's Republic, the time has come: a first passenger plane of China United Airlines has landed on the new mega-airport in the south of the Chinese capital, a special honor for captain and crew.
By 2022, around 45 million passengers are to be handled at the new airport each year. In 2025, it will already be 72 million. China’s President Xi Jinping personally announced the opening of the 450-billion-dollar project. China’s latest prestige project is also called “starfish” because of its six-armed building structure.
The complex has four runways, which are later to be expanded to seven and is equipped with the world’s largest automatic air traffic management system. A new U-new subway transports passengers to downtown Beijing in just 20 minutes.
Already during construction, the Chinese had announced on several occasions that Berlin could learn from Beijing. The opening of the - and much smaller - capital airport in Germany, which has been under construction since 2006, has so far been postponed again and again. In Beijing, on the other hand, a first passenger aircraft landed after only four years.
New supersonic aircraft to fly in 2030: From London to Sydney in four and a half hours
From London to Sydney in just four and a half hours - that's what the UK Space Agency is dreaming of. This is to make this, for the 2030s targeted flight at supersonic pace a special propulsion (Sabre), which the company Reaction Engines from Oxfordshire develops, the U.S. broadcaster quoted the agency. "That's the technology that could definitely do that"
In order to advance the project, the British space agency wants to cooperate more closely with the Australian. A flight from the British capital to the Australian metropolitan metropolis currently takes about 20 hours. As the only supersonic aircraft for passenger transport so far so far, the Concorde from 1976 until the cessation of its operation in 2003 had covered the New York-Paris and took three to three and a half hours for this. In 2000 there was an accident at the take-off of a Concorde at the Paris
"Sabre will make the world smaller with its high-speed point-to-point transport," writes Reaction Engines, among other things, on its homepage. The Sabre drive could be used for efficient travel with a speed of Mach 5 - this means five times as fast as the sound. In addition, such aircraft can also be used outside the Earth's atmosphere and thus enabled the "next generation of truly reusable framing devices".
Uproar in Washington: Trump wants to track down anonymous tipsters in Ukraine affair
In the Ukraine affair over possible abuse of power of the U.S. President, Donald Trump wants to track down possible leaks in the White House.
"I want to know whore the whistleblower has given the information," Trump told U.S. envoy staff at the United Nations in New York on Thursday. The person-inflicted was almost "a spy" and dealt with such in the past "a bit differently" than they are today.
This was seen as an intimidation attempt against tipsters. The event was actually not public. However, several U.S. media spread audio recordings from the address on Friday. Democrats reacted indignantly to the president's statement.
Democrats in the U.S. House of Representatives on Tuesday announced preparations for impeachment proceedings against Trump. Their accusations are based primarily on the written complaint of an anonymous intelligence official who makes serious accusations against Trump and his government headquarters. In response to great public pressure, the whistleblower’s descriptions had been made public on Thursday.
As part of his work, the whistleblower is said to have received information from several government employees, according to which the U.S. president "uses the power of his office" to ensure that another country interferes in his favor in the U.S. election 2020.
At the center of the allegations is a controversial phone call between Trump and Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky at the end of July, in which Trump encouraged his counterpart to investigations that could harm his political rival Joe Biden. This is about previous dealings of Biden’s son Hunter in Ukraine and alleged efforts to protect his offspring from the Ukrainian judiciary. Biden is in the race for the Democratic presidency
The whistleblower also accused the White House with regard to the Ukraine phone call of cover-up: Leading government employees had tried to keep the exact wording of the conversation under the roof. As usual, an electronic wording protocol had been drawn up. After the conversation on the instructions of lawyers from the White House, this had been removed from a usually designated database and was instead stored in a particularly protected system
The identity of the whistleblower is not publicly known. The New York Times reported that it was an employee of the foreign intelligence agency CIA.
On Friday, Trump again questioned the credibility of the source. In a series of tweets on the issue, the president wrote, among other things, that the "so-called whistleblower" had disseminated second-hand information that turned out to be inaccurated. Therefore, there may be no typists or spies at all.
As Trump spoke out in New York about those who are said to have leaked information to the whistleblower, however, sparked outrage. U.S. U.S. House of Representatives chairwoman Nancy Pelosi reacted unsettled and lamented that Trump’s statement once again undermines the integrity of the administration.
Various investigations have beened in the House of Representatives for months on various committees against Trump and his environment. In view of the Ukraine accusations, the Democrats are nowarding investigations with renewed force to initiate a possible impeachment trial against Trump.
"The whistleblower has given us a roadmap for our investigation," the head of the intelligence committee in the House of Representatives, Adam Schiff. Trump sharply attacked Schiff, accused him of a misrepresentation of things and again demanded the resignation of the committee chairman. Trump regularly berates ship and had demanded its withdrawal months ago.
More than 300 former U.S. security and foreign policy officials, who mostly worked under Democratic, but also under Republican presidents, backed the Democrat-launched investigation into possible impeachment in a statement on Friday. Should the accusations come true, that would be "an unscrupulous abuse of power," Trump said.
There is no exact timetable for the further procedure. With their majority in the House of Representatives, the Democrats could initiate a so-called impeachment procedure. However, the decision on an actual impeachment was made in the Senate, where Trump's Republicans have the majority. The prospects for success of such a procedure are therefore low. So far, no U.S. president has been relieved by an impeachment proceeding.
VW works council: Jobs to be cut 'sneakingly'
VW’s plans to further reduce costs in production could come at the expense of manufacturing in Germany and Europe. This concern is driven by the carmaker’s works council. The brand wants to reduce its annual factory costs of ten billion euros by two billion euros by 2023, of which 500 million euros are to be saved as early as this year.
A key to this are the personnel costs. The "Future Pact", negotiated between the company and the works council, which is negotiated between companies and the works council, envisages the reduction of 14,000 jobs in Germany alone by 2020. In principle, a job can only be dismantled from the car manufacturer if it is virtually proven that the associated activities are no longer necessary. In addition, employment protection applies until 2029.
As our newspaper has now learned from works council circles, however, jobs in production, which would actually not be up for disposition, are "creepingly" dismantled via a different route - i.e. past the official course. So vacant regular positions would often not be refilled.
In these cases, the works council has no possibility of forcing reoccupancy. Meanwhile, in many places there is a lack of personnel, the densification of work is increasing, the worries about a gradual bleeding of the vehicle plants would be greater. This would also increase the fear that vehicle production could gradually be withdrawn from Germany and Europe, because it is acceptable in other parts of the world.
The VW brand wants to standardize production even more worldwide and to centrally manage new projects. This is also intended to reduce costs and accelerate decisions - behind it is the goal to increase productivity and thus the competitiveness. Andreas Tostmann said at a managerial conference in Berlin: "Especially in uncertain times from Brexit to the looming trade war between the USA and China, we are well advised to make ourselves crisis-proof".
There are already successes on this way: For example, this year, for the first time since 2013, the production costs per vehicle would decrease compared to the previous year. Torstmann told reporters that the production time for the Golf 8, which is to be unveiled in Wolfsburg on 24 October, would be shortened by 1.1 hours. Exact figures on the production duration VW does not mention, according to information from our newspaper, it is around the Golf 7
20 hours.
In our region, Wolfsburg’s main plant is the only VW factory in which vehicles are produced. The Braunschweig and Salzgitter plants manufacture components. For Wolfsburg, VW’s rule of thumb is that 800,000 cars have to be built there every year so that the plant is satisfactorily profitable. In 2018, however, utilisation in Wolfsburg was only around 700,000 vehicles – due to the declining diesel demand and the problems in the changeover
As production board Tostmann announced last year, however, one million cars are to roll off the assembly line in Wolfsburg. This is to make the bundling of golf production in the main plant possible - because the Zwickau plant is handing off golf production through the conversion to the site for e-car production.
In addition, the SUV Tiguan and Tarraco (Seat) are produced in Wolfsburg. However, the sales figures of Golf and Tiguan have recently declined declining. Therefore, the expectations are now resting on the Golf 8. Despite all the efforts to build e-mobility for the time being, he is to remain the bread-and-butter car of Wolfsburg.
Forced into prostitution with voodoo: Traffickers condemned
Young women from Nigeria have been oated, smuggled into the Ruhr area and forced into prostitution. Six alleged traffickers have to go to prison for this.
Six suspected traffickers from Nigeria were sentenced to probation and imprisonment of up to eight years and three months in Bochum on Friday. The defendants had smuggled young women from Nigeria into the Ruhr area and forced them into prostitution. In order to force their obedience, Nigerian women willing to leave had to make a ritual oathment in front of a voodoo priest.
The victims were between 17 and 22 years old. They were brought across the Mediterranean with the help of Libyan smugglers. However, the dream of a better future was not fulfilled. Arriving in Germany, they were distributed to various brothels in order to process the alleged smuggling costs of an average of 25 000 euros. The wages were therefore taken away from them completely.
The defendants - four men and two women - had admitted smuggling and the subsequent distribution of the young Nigerians to brothels in the trial. "The work made the women physically and psychologically difficult to deal," Judge Isabel Hoffmann said at the sentencing hearing. "Some were properly disgusted."
The verdicts are on commercial and gang-like infiltration of foreigners, human trafficking, pimping and severe forced prostitution.
Ufo calls for negotiations with Lufthansa
In the long-term quarrel with the Lufthansa Group, the flight attendant union UFO has once again threatened strikes.
If the company does not return to the negotiating table, the union "will inform on 14 October about the industrial action measures in the LH Group," Ufo Tariff CEO Daniel Flohr announced on Friday. Lufthansa and its subsidiary Eurowings have been refusing for months to conduct collective bargaining negotiations with Ufo. It is also about the question of whether the union has lawfully terminated collective bargaining agreements at Lufthansa.
On this point, Ufo sees itself strengthened after a decision of the employment tribunal Frankfurt. Lufthansa wanted to determine that the collective agreements had not been terminated. The court dismissed the motions. The company upheld the decision and is considering appeals against it. "That a second court sees the matter completely differently, I deem unlikely," Ufo board member Flohr said.
In August, Lufthansa had also filed an application with the State Labour Court of Frankfurt in order to have the status of Ufo as a trade union reviewed in court. The company argues that the union has not used a representative board for months and is therefore not able to fill out its role as a collective bargaining partner.
This was preceded by a fierce leadership dispute in the trade union, during which numerous members of the board as well as the advisory board have resigned, including the long-standing union leader Nicoley Baublies. Due to the doubts about their leadership, Ufo had suspended an already begun vote on strikes by the cabin crew at Eurowings at the end of July.
Facilitated entry: Saudi Arabia announces new visa system
Until now, Saudi Arabia hasn’t exactly been known as an international tourist magnet. But this is now to change. The country in the Persian Gulf wants to introduce new visa regulations.
With a new visa system, Saudi Arabia wants to attract international tourists in the future. By 2030, they want to increase the number of international and domestic visitors to 100 million per year, the tourism commission said.
"The opening of Saudi Arabia to international tourists is a historical moment for our country," Ahmad Al-Chatib, chairman of the Tourism Commission, said.
Saudi Arabia is difficult for tourists so far. Visas are usually only available for short business trips, religious pilgrimages or visitors with relatives in the desert state. With the new rules, the arch-conservative kingdom is hoping for more revenue from the tourism business. According to its own statement, Riyadh wants to reduce its dependence on the oil trade.
In mid-September, several missiles and drones had hit two of the most important oil facilities in eastern Saudi: the country’s largest oil refinery in Abkaik as well as the Churais oil field. As a result, national oil production collapsed drastically, skyrocketing oil prices. It is likely to take several weeks for the kingdom’s oil production to reach its old level again.
Justin and Hailey Bieber stuck in preparations for wedding party
Singer Justin Bieber (25) and his wife Hailey (22) are preparing for their wedding party. The model posted several party photos on Thursday (local time) in her Instagram Stories, where she can be seen in the white wedding look. Reality star Kendall Jenner (23) and others had organized a bachelorette party in Los Angeles, according to People magazine.
Justin posted several colorful suits printed with bananas on Instagram and asked his followers: "Help me choose my tuxedo for the wedding". According to medial reports, the celebrity couple wants to marry ecclesiastically next weekend.
The Biebers gave themselves the “yes” in a New York registry office a year ago. There had been not a bigger party at the time.
In an interview with Australian Vogue, Hailey recently explained that after initial problems, the two had found a common level: "If you don't want to compromise, you can't have a relationship."
Two killed in plane crash in Czech Republic
Two people have died in the crash of a small plane in the west Czech Republic. The wreck was discovered on Friday in a forest area near the city of Mecin (Mechin), the police said. The dead was a 38-year-old woman and a 64-year-old man.
Authorities had been searching for the machine since Thursday evening after the crew issued an emergency signal via GPS. In the search, a military helicopter was also in use. The cause of the accident has yet to be determined. The crash site is around 30 kilometers south of Pilsen (Plzen) and 100 kilometers northeast of Regensburg.
Half of European tree species are threatened with extinction
In addition to pests, diseases, alien species, unsustainable logging and the growth of the cities are also causes of the threat, the World Nature Conservation Union IUCN reported on Friday in Brussels. It has examined the stocks of all 454 tree species known in Europe. 265 of them occur on no other continent. Of these 265, 58 percent are threatened. 66 are already at the highest level of endangerance and thus from extinction.
The IUCN divides endangered species into six categories: from "potentially endangered" to "endangered", "highly endangered", "endangered" and "extinct in nature" to "extinct". In total, it lists more than 28,000 animal and plant species as endangered worldwide.
Boar anzage and wild horse chestnut threatened
In Germany, boar ash (Sorbus) are threatened, one of the authors, David Allen, told the German Press Agency. "Most are threatened, because the populations are small anyway or due to the conversion or weakening of their habitats".
It also looks bad for the ferrior horse chestnuts. They have been considered "endangered" since 2017, stage two of the IUCN scale. Biggest message represents the Balkan minier moth in addition to a fungus. It has been spreading from south-eastern Europe across the entire continent since the 1980s and has hardly any natural enemies. IUCN estimates the number of wild horse chestnuts
Shrub species and mosses at risk as well
The IUCN scientists simultaneously produced new European red lists for molluscs, shrubs and mosses. More than a fifth of molluscs and mosses and almost halves of all shrub species are threatened, according to the IUCN. This is mainly due to the destruction of the wilderness, introduced species, agricultural practices and climate change. Molluscs such as snails played a key role in recycling nutrients
"This report shows that the situation is precarious for many overlooked and underestimated species, species that form the backbone of Europe's ecosystem and contribute to a healthy planet," said Luc Bas, IUCN director of the Europe office.
Earthquake off Istanbul: 34 injuries reported
34 people have been injured in the most recent earthquake in Turkey. There have been no deaths, according to the data. Dozens of people had to leave their homes, 14 schools in Istanbul have been closed for security reasons, Turkish Vice President Fuat Oktay said on Friday.
Hundreds of buildings were therefore damaged. Since the quake the previous day, there have been almost 200 aftershocks. The 5.8 magnitude earthquake had shaken the Marmara region off Istanbul on Thursday and triggered panic in the metropolis of millions.
Now the government wants to set up a commission to better prepare the country for earthquakes in the future, Oktay announced. Over the past 20 years, Turkey has not been sufficiently prepared for quakes, Istanbul Mayor Ekrem Imamoglu said.
Under the Marmara Sea lies a section of the so-called North Anatolian disturbance, which marks the border between the Eurasian and Anatolian slabs.
One of the heaviest quakes was one of magnitude 7.6 near Istanbul in 1999. The epicenter at that time was located in Gölcük southeast of Istanbul. More than 17 000 people died.
Already on Tuesday, a 4.6 magnitude quake had rocked the region around Istanbul. Authorities called on residents to stay away from damaged buildings.
Super Bowl 2020: Jennifer Lopez (JLo) and Shakira perform on halftime show
Latin pop singers Jennifer Lopez and Shakira will lead the half-time show of the next American football final Super Bowl. That's what the singers announced on Twitter. "It won't get bigger," Shakira wrote to a photo of herself and Lopez. She's insanely excited to stand on the Super Bowl stage. On February 2, 2020, it's going to be in Miami.
Jennifer Lopez explained that she had dreamed of performing at the Super Bowl since 1996 - at that time she saw Diana Ross flying into the sky at the half-time show. "Now it's even special because the NFL lasts 100 years and I'm going to perform with another Latina." She couldn't wait "to show what we girls can do on the biggest stage in the world."
Shakira pointed out in a statement that she was honored to stand next to another artist on one of the biggest stages in the world and to represent Latinos and Latinas from the USA and all over the world. "And then also on my birthday!" The main sponsor of the event wrote on Twitter that it was the first time that the two "queens" stand together on a stage.
Shakira is from Colombia. Jennifer Lopez was born in New York, her parents are from Puerto Rico.
Fight against declining number of spectators
The Super Bowl is the highlight of the American football season. The game and the halftime show are among the most watched TV broadcasts in the U.S. Even if you've recently dropped viewer numbers: this February, the spectacle in the U.S. lured the fewest viewers for more than ten years. Nevertheless, it was still almost a hundred million people on average.
There had been turmoil about the half-time show of the past Super Bowl in the run-up: In the dispute over the handling of quarterback Colin Kaepernick, according to media reports last February, top stars such as Rihanna, Pink and Cardi B did not want to perform. After all, the pop group Maroon 5 led the half-time show - the reviews were unfriendly.
Kaepernick, then quarterbaker of the San Francisco 49ers, had not got to his knees in the 2016/17 season before NFL games at the national anthem in protest against police violence and racism. With his protest, Kaepernick sparked a movement, numerous players took over the gesture. This caused a lot of excitement, U.S. President Donald Trump attacked the mostly African-American football stars sharply.
Cook bankruptcy: Hotels get money from insurance
Some banks are likely to find the bankruptcy of the travel provider Thomas Cook according to a press reportation. There is a threat of high depreciation on outstanding bonds and loans. Credit Suisse is also affected by this, writes the "Financial Times" in its issue on Friday.
Banks such as Barclays, Morgan Stanley, UniCredit or Credit Suisse would probably have to make a total write-down on their books amounting to up to 1.8 billion British pounds (around 2 billion euros), according to the report
The creditors of bonds would have to accept depreciations of 900 million to 1 billion pounds and the lenders between 550 and 825 million pounds. The amounts actually to be depreciated are more likely to come at the upper end of the bandwidths, an expert told the paper.
How big the losses of the creditors will actually be in the end, will become apparent after the expiry of the insolvency proceedings. A person close to this procedure did not spray too much hope. For example, the approximately 40 aircraft from Thomas Cook in the UK are "old" and many of the properties are in bad locations. The most money can probably be recovered with the sale of the airport slots as well as the Thomas Cook brand, it said.
Rackete is said to have brought suspected murderers to Italy
Sea-Watch captain Carola Rackete may have brought men to Italy who are said to have tortured and killed people in Libya. "We can't rule that out, but don't have backed-up information on it themselves," Sea-Watched spokesman Ruben Neugebauer told the dpa news agency.
Other migrants had recognized the three men in Italy. They are accused of raping, torturing and killing people in Libya. The alleged perpetrators had been arrested in the reception camp in Italy's Messina. Neugebauer stressed that the refugee organizations could not verify who they left on their ships. "They come without passports".
Carola Rackete had illegally taken migrants to the port of Lampedusa on June 29, ramming a ship of the Italian financial police. On that day, the three suspects are also said to have arrived there
U.S. warns federal government against Huawei
U.S. Federal Communications Commission (FCC) chief Ajit Pai has warned the federal government against trusting Chinese networker Huawei.
We believe that the risks are too great," Pai told Handelsblatt, referring to the construction of the new 5G mobile network.
China has a "national security law" requiring every company "under Chinese jurisprudence" to obligate instructions from the security bodies.
The current law also forbids companies to inform third parties about such inquiries," the FCC chief said in a statement. This makes it very difficult to have confidence in the integrity of technical equipment and services.
The federal government, meanwhile, has agreed to demand a "declaration of confidence" from suppliers.
Berlin initially wants to try to control the risks, for example with technical solutions, reports the "Handelsblatt", citing its own information.
Pai doubts that this is possible. The 5G technology does not only consist of the hardware:
Especially the software is important to keep the devices up-to-date. This software consists of billions of rows of program code that change over time," Pai said.
"It's very hard to imagine" that a government agency, "no matter where, would be able to review every single update in real-time in order to track down security risks," the FCC chief said.
He accuses the Chinese company Huawei of close contacts with the Chinese state apparatus:
The ownership relationships are opaque. The employee company to which supposedly owns the company is not necessarily independent of the government," Pai told Handelsblatt.
According to the U.S., there are findings according to which government employees are placed within the company. Pai was recently for talks in Berlin to promote the position of the U.S.
Chemical factory in northern France burns: Schools closed
At night, residents hear explosions. A gigantic wall of fire illuminates the sky above a northern French chemical factory. The blaze is extinguished after hours - but the clean-up work could now take days.
After a fire at a chemical plant in the northern French city of Rouen on Thursday, schools and crèches in the region will remain closed.
In twelve municipalities, the facilities would not open as a precautionary on Friday as a precaution, the responsible prefecture said. The Interior Ministry had urged citizens to stay in buildings as much as possible and not expose themselves to the smoke. There were no injuries. Farmers and breeders were called upon to bring their animals indoors, protecting their feed and stopping the harvest for the time being.
The blaze could be extinguished completely on Thursday evening, as the prefecture announced. There is still the risk that smell spreads after extinguishing, according to the prefecture on Twitter. According to the prefecture, 240 firefighters as well as 90 polices and gendarmes were on duty at the scene.
A gigantic black column of smoke had previously risen from the chemical factory. French media reported huge flames and explosions at the Lubrizol's factory, which manufactures additives for oils. The production facility is located a few kilometers from the city center.
According to the authorities, the Normandy factory belongs to the so-called Seveso category of hazardous sites, which are particularly supervised. A devastating chemical accident occurred in 1976 in Seveso near Milan.
Pierre-André Durand Prefect Pierre-André Durand told BFMTV that there had initially been no "high toxicity" detected in samples. However, pollution of the nearby Seine could not be ruled out. In the river, dams had been built against the pollution, the prefecture explained.
The blaze broke out in the early morning at about 02.40 a.m
French Finance Minister: "Germany must invest now"
French Economy and Finance Minister Bruno Le Maire has called on Germany for greater investment to boost economic growth in the eurozone.
Germany has to invest now, the sooner the better," Le Maire said on Thursday evening at the presentation of the French budget for the coming year.
The economy in the eurozone is weakening, the minister warned.
We must not wait until the economic situation worsens in order to make the necessary decisions.”
Action would need those states that would have room for more investment, Le Maire said, among others, with a view to Germany.
France does not have these margins, the minister added, pointing to his country's high national debt. This stands at more than 98 per cent of gross domestic product. French public debt must be "stabilised" and "lowered," Le Maire said.
In recent years, appeals have repeatedly come from France to make more public investment in order to enliven the European economy. In return, German politicians have repeatedly urged France to get to grips with its budget deficit.
The French budget for 2020 presented on Thursday envisages tax relief of more than nine billion euros for households.
Among other things, the French government is responding to the protests of the Yellow Vest movement. New debt is expected to fall to 2.2 percent, according to expected 3.1 percent this year.
Is he still trying to trick?: Johnson dodges question about Brexit tactics
The British Prime Minister Johnson sharply criticizes the "capitulation law" passed by parliament.
Parliament is back, a law prescribes Britain's prime minister to avoid a chaos Brexit. However, critics fear Boris Johnson could still take advantage of a loophole. Asked about this possibility, however, the Tory politician dodges.
British Prime Minister Boris Johnson remains covered about whether he wants to enforce an unregulated Brexit - despite the last law passed by parliament, which is intended to prevent exactly that. In response to a corresponding question, Johnson told reporters only that his government would abide by applicable law.
The law stipulates that the government must request a postponement of the withdrawal date if no agreement is reached with the EU by mid-October. Johnson criticizes this as a "capitulation law" that complicates negotiations with the EU.
Former British Prime Minister John Major on Thursday had expressed the presumption that Johnson could try to use "political harassment" to circumvent the law. The Premier could use a procedural trick that postpones the entry into force of the law on the post-EU exit. Johnson said he was seeking a contract with the European Union. "We are confident of leaving the EU on 31 October, and the best way is with an agreement."
Johnson's chief adviser Dominic Cummings, meanwhile, explained that he perceives the political disputes over Brexit as a "walk in the park" and expressed confidence that the desired EU exit will still come about: "We're enjoying this, we're going to win, we're not going to leave the EU, don't worry," Cummings told Britain's Telegraph newspaper.
Cummings, who led the "Vote Leave" Brexit campaign during the election campaign before the 2016 EU referendum, is now acting as chief strategist in Britain's Downing Street headquarters. He is said to be behind the uncompromising policy that led the Premier into an almost hopeless situation.
"The Polaroid Diaries" by Linda McCartney
Jimi Hendrix, Bob Dylan or Janis Joplin - they were all in front of Linda McCartney's camera, which has become one of the very big rock photographers. But it doesn't always have to be the big glamour. The photo book "The Polaroid Diaries" (pockets) delves deeply into the McCartney family's very private world.
Paul McCartney, who was married to her from 1969 until Linda's death in 1998, was visibly proud and stirred when he presented the pictorial volume "The Polaroid Diaries" in mid-September at the Victoria and Albert Museum in London. The illustrious guests also included Ringo Starr, Stella McCartney, Olivia Harrison and Pretenders frontwoman Chrissie Hynde.
"#ThePolaroidDiaries is a glimpse into the life of an extraordinary family from the time before Instagram, a celebration for the legacy of LindaMcCartney as a dedicated artist and the instant magigy of the Polaroid film."
Linda McCartney, who had studied art history at the University of Aizona until she found photography, the camera was always there. Thousands of Polaroids made it, a few hundred of which are now published for the first time in "The Polaroid Diaries."
It's portraits, still lifes, landscapes, several hamsters, a sheep in the kitchen and other interiors from the early 70s to the late 90s. "She wanted real moments," writes art critic Ekow Eshun in his foreword. And, of course, there's always one in the spotlight: her husband Paul - as you may have never seen him before.
There he dances with his daughter Mary on his shoulders, on the phone with a bag on his head in a bathrobe or wears naughty moonboots. Sure, the McCartneys were an extraordinary family, but beyond the spotlight on their farm in southern England or in Scotland also led a normal life. So, according to the BBC, Mary McCartney said that she would have slept in a bed as a baby
Like hardly any other medium, the Poloraid camera captures the immediate moment. Linda McCartney’s shots are certainly a kind of family diary, but they’re very much more than pure snapshots.
It's about the decisive click: you have to recognize when a great photo happens in front of you. And then you have to snap it at exactly the right moment," said Paul McCartney. "If you snap two seconds later or two seconds sooner, it can be a completely different photo." And Linda would have had the ability to always capture just the right moment. "She just knew that she had it," Paul said in the Eshun essay.
The light, the composition, the excerption and the colors are right, which not only makes "The Polaroid Diaries" an entertaining picture book for Beatles fans, but in addition to everything anecdotal is also a great aesthetic pleasure, where landscapes also look like abstract paintings. The perfect addition to the newly released Beatles album "Abbey Road", which appeared exactly 50 years ago.
“We’re going to keep buying oil and gas from Iran
Turkey wants to continue importing oil and gas from the neighboring country despite U.S. sanctions imposed on Iran. Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan said in an interview published on Friday that Turkey could not cut off oil and gas import from Iran.
We will continue to buy our natural gas from there," Erdogan said in New York, where he had attended the U.N. General Assembly.
Erdogan admitted, however, that Turkey had problems importing Iranian petroleum as some companies "withdrew because of U.S. threats."
Even so, Turkey "will continue our relations with Iran, especially on this issue and on many other issues," said the Turkish president, who had repeatedly criticized U.S. sanctions against Iran in the past.
The U.S. has pursued a policy of "maximum pressure" towards the Islamic Republic since its withdrawal from the international nuclear agreement with Iran. According to the U.S., Iran is secretly pursuing a nuclear program and does not abide by the deal.
Their sanctions have led to the slump in Iranian oil exports and a severe economic crisis in Iran. In recent months, tensions in the Gulf region have intensified considerably.
U.S. government vows to restrict admission of refugees
The U.S. government wants to drastically restrict the reception of refugees. The U.S. State Department declared on Thursday that the annual ceiling of a resettlement program should be lowered from currently 30,000 to 18,000 refugees. This corresponds to a cut of 40 percent.
The State Department stated that while the U.S. remains committed to supporting refugees, the most important task is to protect and serve U.S. citizens. Currently, the U.S. immigration system is overburdened.
In the future, the U.S. government wants to focus on accepting two refugee groups: people persecuted because of their faith - and Iraqis who have helped the U.S. military in the country and are thus in danger, according to the State Department's statement. From Honduras, Guatemala and El Salvador, on the other hand, a maximum of 1500 refugees are to be taken in.
In the programme launched in 1980, refugees from U.N. warehouses around the world will be selected and re-seated in the U.S. The cap of 18,8,000 refugees for the 2020 budget year would be the lowest in the program. Even the currently applicable cap of 30,000 is a low. When U.S
Aid agencies reacted horrifiedly to the announced lowering of the ceiling. "This is a very sad day for America," the head of the International Resurrection Committee, David Miliband, said. The U.S. would thus further damage its leadership role in protecting the world's most vulnerable people.
Trump is trying to strictly limit immigration to the U.S. - legal as well as illegally. The U.S. government expects 350,000 asylum applications for the coming budget year. These figures are separate from the resettlement program.
Donald Trump - "Comes close to a snitch"
U.S. President Donald Trump has accused whistleblowers in the whistleblower affair of espionage. Whoever ensnaged corresponding clues to the informant who accuses him of abuse of power "comes close to a snitch," Trump said, according to a recording of a private event on Thursday in New York. That's what several U.S. medians report in agreement.
According to this, Trump spoke in front of employees of the American U.N. mission. When he was asked about the person who informed the whistleblower about disputed events in the White House, he replied: “That comes close to a snitch”. Then Trump added: “Do you know what we did in old times when we were smart? Properity. The spies and treason, we used to deal with it a bit differently now.”
Observers evaluated the latter remark on a possible reference to the death penalty. The Democrats drew sharp criticism of Trump's remarks and accused him of intimidation of witnesses. It was also an attempt to obstruct a congressial investigation into a possible impeachment against him, wrote the chairmen of the committees for foreign affairs, intelligence agencies and oversight in the House of Representatives.
The whistleblower accuses the president, in a now-published complaint, of having abused his office to solicit interference from another country in the 2020 U.S. election. Specifically, it concerns, among other things, his controversial phone call with his Ukrainian counterpart Volodymyr Zelensky in the summer: In the conversation, Trump encouraged him to initiate investigations against his possible democratic challenger Joe Biden and his son
It is still unclear who the whistleblower itself and its informants are. However, as the New York Times reports, the whistleblower is said to be an analyst of the foreign intelligence agency CIA.
Refugee boat capsized: baby and toddlers drown in Aegean
Again and again, people try to come over the Aegean Sea from Turkey to Greece. The often much too small boats regularly capsize. In the demise of an inflatable boat, seven migrants have now died - including five children.
Seven migrants have died off the Greek island of Chios during the sinking of an inflatable boat. Among the fatalities are a baby and four other children, the Greek coast guard reported. Four children, three women and five men were able to be rescued from the floodwaters by the crew of a passing ferry.
Among the casualties is also said to be a smuggler, Chio's local news portal Alithia.gr. The migrants had departed from Turkey in the morning. They had tried to reach the small archipelago Inousses in the north-east of the island of Chios. Inousses lies between Chios and the Turkish mainland.
It is not yet known from which countries the migrants come. Currently, hundreds of migrants from Turkey move to the Greek islands in the east of the Aegean Sea, mostly in much too small boats. Last year, 174 people died on this route, according to the United Nations Refugee Agency (UNHCR).
The Who cancel concert
A U.S. concert of the rock band The Who has come to an abrupt end because of an adversity of lead singer Roger Daltrey. In the middle of the appearance on Wednesday night (local time) in Houston, his voice failed the service. At the eighth song, Daltrey told the audience: "I think I should leave it while it's still running".
Lead guitarist Pete Townsend apologized and added that Daltrey "can't speak right now". Townsend promised the audience to get their money's worth at a reparation concert, but an appointment has not yet been set.
Performances scheduled for Friday in Dallas and for Sunday in Denver canceled The Who. They are due to catch up at a later date. 75-year-old Daltrey and his 74-year-old bandmate Townsend are the only remaining founding members. The British cult band formed 55 years ago.
Rental suddenly discontinued: WeWork lets real estate markets tremble
In New York, WeWork has risen to become the largest office tenant.
Within a few years, WeWork rises from a small startup to a driving force on the world’s most expensive real estate markets. But now the company is in crisis. Landlords have to fear for billions in revenues.
The crisis of the office landlord WeWork threatens to disturb the balance in the largest real estate markets in the world. The company had decommissioned founder Adam Neumann a few days ago as boss, postponed the planned IPO and started with the sale of subsidiaries and values such as the private jet for the executives in order to gain financial scope for the coming weeks and months. In addition, the British "Financial Times", WeWork has suddenly stopped the conclusion of new rentals
WeWork rents large office spaces for the long term and rents them to members at short notice in partly small units. This can be individual freelancers, small startups or entire departments of large corporations. So far, WeWork burns money at breathtaking speed with this business. Last year, the company recorded a loss of 1.6 billion dollars, with a turnover of 1.8 billion dollars. This year alone, the rental costs are said to have been increased by the rental of ever new large areas
According to the Financial Times, WeWork has meanwhile entered into lease agreements with maturities of up to 15 years, resulting in commitments of more than 40 billion dollars. These huge liabilities could become a problem not only for the company itself and its investors, but for entire real estate markets.
Calculations by Economist Dan Alpert from Cornell Law School in New York according to WeWork in Manhattan, one of the world's most expensive real estate markets, in the past 24 months almost 300,000 square meters of office space. WeWork's new contracts alone ensured that the demand for offices in New York was greater than the newly marketed areas during this time.
Even in the British capital, the balance in the real estate market without a further expansion of the aggressive start-up could shift noticeably. Among others, WeWork is the main tenant, in an office building in the center of the financial metropolis bought last year by the savings bank fund provider Deka for almost 100 million euros.
Many landlords have rebuilt their properties for the requirements of WeWork with high investments. If WeWork fails to make its business model profitable soon, the property owners would probably have to write off part of the hoped-for billions in revenue. Access to the - anyway scarce - reserves of WeWork they do not have despite the long-term leases. Because the rental of the individual locations of WeWork is usually based only by specially founded
Russia again arrested numerous North Korean fishermen
In recent days, a total of 262 poachers have been arrested who illegally fished in the waters, the Russian domestic intelligence service FSB said on Friday.
Only ten days ago, 161 fishermen from North Korea were arrested. Previously, the border guards had been shelled and three of them injured. This time there had been no casualties, it said. Communist North Korea suffers from chronic food shortages.
The North Korean fishermen were therefore on the road in Russian territorial waters, where the coast guard of Russia according to its own information for the protection of its marine biological resources patrols. 30,000 squids were discovered during the renewed checks. In addition, according to the data, three fishing and two motor boats were seized and brought to Nachodka and Posjet near Vladivostok
Even after lawsuits by local entrepreneurs this year, the Russian Fisheries Authority had not issued fishing licenses to the North Koreans. According to the media, thousands of fishermen have already been temporarily arrested there in 2019.
The head of the unification of Russian fishing companies in the Far Eastern region of Primorye, Georgi Martynov, recently also complained of high ecological damage caused by North Korean fishermen. They fished with close-knit synthetic nets banned in Russia. With these, even small creatures would be retrieved from the sea. In addition, the nets would simply be disposed of in the sea, where they would take 200 years
According to Moscow, three members of the Russian coast guard had been wounded in the previous week’s arrest. According to the FSB, a likewise wounded North Korean later died from his injuries. On September 17, Russian border guards first arrested a North Korean boat and then a second one after it opened fire.
U.S. imposes travel ban on ex-president Castro
The U.S. has imposed travel bans on former Cuban President Raúl Castro and his children. The U.S. State Department justified this in a statement on Thursday with Castro's involvement in "rough human rights violations." As head of the Communist Party of the Caribbean state, he presided over a system in which thousands of Cubans are arbitrarily arrested and there are more than 100 political prisoners.
As chairman of the armed forces, the brother of the deceased revolutionary leader Fidel Castro is also complicit in human rights violations there through the support of the president, Nicolás Maduro. "The disregard for human rights and the use of violence by the Cuban regime in order to support the former Maduro regime are responsible for the ongoing crises in Cuba and in Venezuela," U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo tweeted.
On Wednesday, President Donald Trump issued travel bans against members of the Venezuelan government and the military. In Venezuela, a bitter power struggle between the government and the opposition has been raging for months. The U.S. has imposed a whole series of sanctions on Maduro's government to bring him to his knees and help the opposition leader and self-proclaimed interim president Juan Guaidó to power.
Cuba is considered one of Maduro’s closest allies. The socialist Caribbean island receives oil from Venezuela in exchange for the services of posted doctors. According to the U.S. and the Venezuelan opposition, Cuba has also sent numerous intelligence agents to Venezuela to help the government in the suppression of anti-government.
New climate protests from Fridays for Future
A week after the biggest international climate protests to date, thousands of people want to take to the streets again today for more climate protection.
At the end of an international climate strike week, the Fridays for Future movement is once again planning promotions and rallies in dozens of countries around the world. New Zealand and Australia are likely to get started because of the time difference before the protests over Europe to North America. Climate activist Greta Thunberg wanted to take part in a large rally in Montreal, Canada. Protesters also wanted to gather in front of the White House in Washington.
How big the worldwide support will be this Friday after the record turnout of the previous week is relatively unclear. Thunberg itself was good things. "Last Friday, over four million people went on strike for the climate. This Friday we do it again!" she wrote on Twitter. So far, there have been 6383 actions in 170 countries as part of the strike week, she added.
Especially in Germany, the influx last Friday was very large. A good third of all worldwide protesters - around 1.4 million - had participated in the protests according to estimates by Fridays for Future in 575 German cities. This time, according to a listing of the rallies in at least 65 cities are planned. Among them in metropolises such as Berlin, Hamburg, Munich and Cologne, but also in smaller communities and cities such as Jüchen in North Rhine-Westphalia or Westerland
At the age of 15, Thunberg had put himself in front of the Swedish parliament in August 2018 in order to call on the politicians of their country for a stronger commitment to climate protection. Within a short time, the Fridays-for-Future movement has emerged. The climate protests are mainly attended by pupils and students, but increasingly also scientists and other adults.
Together, they are calling for more ambition from politics in the fight against global heating and the looming climate catastrophe. Above all, according to the Paris climate agreement, global warming must be curbed to below 1.5 degrees compared to pre-industrial times.
Thunberg had arrived in New York almost a month ago after a two-week transatlantic trip with a deep-sea racing yacht. There she had recently participated in climate protests and summits of the United Nations. On Monday, she gave an emotional speech at the UN, in which she urgently addressed the heads of state and government of the earth. In her homethous Stockholm, she was on Wednesday for more climate protection
Russia bans smoke on balcony
The people of Russia will no longer be allowed to smoke on the balcony in the future. Thinks a change in fire protection. "This also applies to smoking," a spokesman for the emergency ministry said on Thursday in Moscow according to the agency Tass. The ban is to come into force on 1 October.
Those who still smoke must expect a fine of 3,000 rubles (42.46 euros). If the cigarette ignites a fire, then the penalty is higher. According to the ban, burning candles are covered by the probe. Grilling meat skewers on the balcony is no longer allowed.
Beating cybercriminals - Investigators excrete servers
In the fight against crimes on the Internet, German investigators for the first time dug up a large server provider for illegal transactions on the dark web.
A total of 13 perpetrators are suspected of having hosted numerous websites through which internationally active criminals operated their machinations in a former Nato bunker in Traben-Trarbach an der Moselle.
This was announced by the attorney general's office in Koblenz. Seven suspects were arrested during a major action on Thursday evening. The darknet is a shielded part of the internet.
Customers of the suspects are said to have been the operators of the world's second-largest darknet marketplace for drugs, "Wall Street Market" - which investigators had smashed in the spring. The attack on 1.25 million telecom routers at the end of November 2016 was also steered, according to the attorney general's office via a server in the cyber bunker.
Such a hoster was excavated for the first time in Germany, it said. A web hoster provides the infrastructure for an Internet presence.
During the access campaign with the support of special forces such as the GSG9, there have been searches in Germany and in neighboring countries. It was the result of almost five years of long investigations. At noon, the investigators want to inform about further details.
New worldwide climate protests from Fridays for Future
A week after the biggest international climate protests so far, thousands of people want to take to the streets again today for more climate protection.
At the end of an international climate strike week, the Fridays for Future movement once again planned actions and rallies in dozen countries worldwide.
Protests are planned in Germany in at least 65 cities, including in metropolises such as Berlin, Hamburg, Munich and Cologne, but also in smaller municipalities and cities such as Jüchen in North Rhine-Westphalia or Westerland on Sylt. Climate activist Greta Thunberg wants to take part in a large rally in Montreal.
Climate protests ǀ Who are Thunberg's backers?
Sense of justice and courage are high virtues, even in the 21st century, in a world full of war and smoke factories that warm the climate. All the more astonishing that a 16-year-old girman of all people of that time holds the mirror in front of their own. But what is behind it? Is it helpfulness or clever marketing of a climate activist?
On the one hand Greta Thunberg puts the crowds in motion and leads young people to rise for a better climate-friendly world. It is historically not the first time in Europe that a young person manages to attract a collection of children and adolescent. Already in 1212, a child named Nikolaus from Cologne felt chosen to advocate for a fairer world. At that time, he was also able
But is there a downside in this regard? Why does it need a share called "We don't have time" to save the world? In addition, Greta Thunberg has rich PR managers at her side. By the way, her mother has published a book, which has sold far-reachingly, thanks to the daughter in the limelight. Even tried-and-tested followers of Greta Thunberg were a little indignant
Here the spirits are now divided: Is it morally right to enrich themselves financially when saving the world? Is it still authentic if certain circles of people work together with stockbrokers during the world rescue action? No matter which position is ultimately taken, a critical and reflected point of view is increasingly important in a democratically shaped society, because democracy thrives on different opinions and discussions. That is whhere it makes quite sense even seemingly pious projects from certain trends
Comet discovered: Interstellar guest crushed through our solar system
A foreign comet thwarts our solar system at the moment.
The assumptions of the astronomers have been confirmed: a foreign comet thwarts our solar system at the moment - it is only the second of its kind.
Garching/Washington - For a long time, the researchers only suspected it, but now they have certainty: a comet from another star is currently thwarting our solar system. It was baptized 2I/Borisov after its discoverer and is currently employing astronomers worldwide.
2I/Borisov: Comet has been spotted by amateur astronomers in Crimea
The said body was spotted by an amateur astronomer named Gennadi Borissov in Crimea. He glanced to the skies with a self-made telescope and further follow-up observations confirmed his first suspicion. It is actually an unknown comet that does not originate from our solar system. He is only the second of its kind to be sighted during the fly-through.
The great opportunity for astronomers is now to catch insights into an alien solar system from close up. Apparently, the come is similar to those of our own system, as the astrophysical institute of the Canaries reports. The comet, according to the International Astronomical Union (IAC), is clearly of interstellar origin and is currently moving on a so-called hyperbelvish orbit that takes it out of our system.
Comet has already been sighted before approach - and can be observed for several months
But before that time, astronomers can observe it in detail. Because unlike its predecessor, 2I/Borisov has already been sighted in its approach, which means that it can be examined from Earth for many months. "The object will reach the greatest brightness in mid-December and will then still be visible with medium-sized telescopes until April 2020," explains Davide Farnocchia from the U.
The predecessor of the new visitor: 1I/2017 U1 "Oumuamua".
First findings about Comet 2I/Borisov arouse hopes in science
According to the initial findings, the comet is estimated to be 2 to 16 kilometers in diameter. At the same time, the celestial body seems to be similar to our comet - because, as IAC researcher Julia de León puts it, the spectrum of the tail star is similar to ours. This observation suggests that comets in other solar systems could be formed by similar processes as in own, added de León's colleague
Observation, however, goes beyond individual findings. The fact that two such objects have already been discovered within just two years may allow a new way of researching certain processes in other solar systems.
Asteroids repeatedly employ the researchers. Only recently, a giant asteroid raced past the Earth and came dangerously close to it.
'Absolutely reprehensible': Rachel Johnson horrified by brother
Boris Johnson is becoming more lonely. First his younger brother leaves the cabinet, now his sister scourges with clear words the belligerent language of the prime minister as "tasteless". It is not the brother she knows.
"Capitulation," "fraud," treaty": Prime Minister Boris Johnson is under pressure and continues to upgrade - at least verbally. At Question Time in the House of Commons this Wednesday, this encountered massive outragement of the opposition, and meanwhile other critics have spoken out: dozens of bishops and his own sister Rachel Johnson.
The well-known journalist and author told Sky News that her brother used words such as surrender, as if the opponents of an EU exit should be "hanged, slung, quadrupled, tarred and feathered". She thinks this is extremely reprehensible. She does not recognize her brother's version," said Rachel Johnsons, who in the elections to the E-European Parliament for the anti-Brex
In particular, she met with the fact that her brother Jo Cox mentioned, an EU-friendly MP who had been murdered by a neo-Nazi a few days before the exit referendum in 2016. Their memories can best be honored by pulling through Brexit, Boris Johnson had said in the House of Commons. "It is very distasteful to refer to an MP who has been murdered by someone who shouted 'Bri
"It makes me sick when Jo's name is used," he tweeted. The best way to honour her is if one passionately and decisively advocates what one believes - no matter what view you are. But never allowed to "demonize the other side". He also complained that an atmosphere had arisen in which attacks had become more likely.
Only a few weeks ago Boris Johnson's younger brother Jo had resigned his office as secretary of state and his mandate as a Tory deputy. "I've been torn between loyalty to the family and the national interest in the past few months - it's an indissoluble tension," Jo Johnson justified the move.
Johnson's language also met with sharp criticism in the Church. As the Times reports, all 118 bishops and archbishops of the Church of England criticize the language that is "not worthy of this country." In a joint statement, they declared: "In the past few days, the language, both in debates and outside Parliament, has not been acceptable. We should talk to each other with respect."
Shakira and Jennifer Lopez perform at Super Bowl
The NFL makes a strong mark in the year of its 100th anniversary: In the half-time show of the upcoming Super Bowl, two women with Latino roots perform. They promise "an explosion of fun and energy."
Latina power at the Super Bowl: Superstars Shakira (42) and Jennifer Lopez (50) will deliver the half-time show at the upcoming finale of the U.S. football league NFL. That's what the organizers announced Thursday (local time).
"I am incredibly honored and humbled to represent the Latino community alongside JLo. Because this one has an incredible strength in the U.S.," Shakira shared in a video.
The finale of the American football league, which celebrates its 100th birthday next year, will take place on February 2 in Miami. In the metropolis at the southern tip of Florida, many people live with Latin American roots. Shakira ("Waka Waka") comes from Colombia, Lopez ("On the Floor") is puerto-Rican origin.
"I love it for the Super Bowl to put two women on stage this time - and two Latinas," Lopez explained. This sends an important message to all Americans and the world. She promises "an explosion of fun and energy" for the show.
The 50-year-old was born in New York after her parents emigrated from Puerto Rico. In the 2000s, she landed big hits with songs such as "Waiting For Tonight" or "Let's Get Loud" and later also released songs in Spanish. Lopez also works as an actress and can be seen in German cinemas from November in the drama "Hustlers".
Shakira became known in Colombia as the star of a telenovela and singer. She made her worldwide breakthrough in 2001 with her first bilingual album and the single "Whenever, Whereever."
Announcing half-time guests is eagerly awaited every time in the U.S. This year, many also see the selection as a political gesture. After all, the country is deeply divided, not least due to the immigration policies of President Donall Trump.
The Super Bowl is the largest U.S. sporting event and at the same time a gigantic advertising spectacle. Musicians like Justin Timberlake, Beyoncé, Lady Gaga, Katy Perry, Madonna, Bruno Mars and the Rolling Stones caused a sensation there. As a rule, well over 100 million TV viewers are watching the NFL finale, it is also extremely popular in Germany by now.
Last year there were political controversies in the run-up. The reason was the dispute over quarterback Colin Kaepernick, who had refused in protest against police violence against black people to rise while playing the national anthem and had been harshly approached, among others, by U.S. President Donald Trump. In solidarity with Kaepernick, some musicians had finally criticized a Super Bowl appearance - including Rihanna and Pink
An actor and Youtuber slams Egypt's ruler: For the time being, however, Mohamed Ali prefers to stay abroad.
The two men, who have influence on whether blood flows again on the streets of Egypt, spent the past week both abroad. One of the two hoved back into Cairo airport on Friday. President Abdelfattah al-Sisi returned from the U.N
Misleaded - this, according to the president, the other man who currently determines what is happening on the streets. Mohamed Ali, of course, remained abroad, where he evades the access of the security forces, who would immediately arrest him if he tried to enter Egyptian soil. Weeks ago, the 45-year-old was known as an actor only to a minority of the Egyptians. Today, he is the tone-giving voice
He tackles the head of state directly and in quite rustical language. While the people are starving, the president revels in luxury and letting himself build magnificent new palaces, it is said. The army leadership, on which Sisi bases his rule, accuses Ali of corruption and enrichment. Because in his second profession as a contractor, he did 15 years with exactly these gentlemen, he seems to know quite exactly what he is talking about
After last week, for the first time since Sisi's grip on power, thousands had demonstrated against his rule and almost 2000 citizens, among them prominent figures of civil society, human rights lawyers and opposition politicians, had been arrested, Ali called for a "million march" for this Friday. That really so many people would venture into the streets was unlikely. But there were occasional smaller protests, for example in Giseh near Cairo
From the city of Mansura in the Nile Delta, where protesters tore banners to pieces with the portrait of the president last week, massive police presence was reported on Friday. Security forces built checkpoints in the city and on the outage roads. In Cairo, several subway stations in the centuary were closed due to "maintenance work", most of the streets around the Tahrirplatz, the heart of the revolution of 2011
Mohamed Ali prompted these precautions to change his tactics: "Our goal doesn't have to be Tahrir Square," Ali said into his video camera in Barcelona. "Let's not collide with police officers," but this cannot be understood as a pullback: "All of Egypt is Tahrir Square."
If the provider promises too much performance
If the Internet connection always seems much slower than advertised by the provider, there are various testing options.
If you conclude an Internet contract, you don’t have to blindly trust the speed information of the providers. Customers can check how much of it is actually achieved in the event of abnormalities themselves.
Finally, the previously promised transmission rates are part of the contract and should therefore also be achieved, explains the consumer center Bavaria.
If the connection always seems much slower than advertised by the provider, there are various testing options. Thus, the measuring tools of the Federal Network Agency (BNetzA) measure the actual data transmission rates and compare them with the rate agreed in the contract.
"However, the tests of the providers are carried out according to provider-specific requirements and are therefore not calculated uniformly and objectively," says the consumer centre Bavaria on its side.
If the connection is much slower than announced, customers can set the provider a reasonable period of 14 days in order to produce the promised service. This is best done by those affected in writing.
If this does nothing even after multiple requests, customers can terminate and possibly also claim compensation, but in the absence of success, only the legal action will help.
First Senior Republican for Investigation
As the first senior Republican, the governor of Vermont has advocated an impeachment investigation against U.S. President Donald Trump. "I think the investigation is important, yes, and where that leads from here is determined by the facts that are ascertained," Phil Scott said at a news conference on Thursday. He wanted to know more about the whistleblower affair before action - an impeachment procedure
Scott said he wasn't surprised by revelations that Trump had repeatedly called on Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy in a phone call to investigate interventions by Democratic presidential candidate and former vice president-elect Joe Biden in Ukraine. Because Trump has been observing Biden "for years."
Republicans have so far held back with criticism of Trump over the whistleblower affair; few have shown unease because of it. Scott's remarks are a first sign of this in Trump's party. Scott, a moderate Republican, has already objected several times against Trump's policies, such as easing environmental regulations and tightening immigration regulations.
Spider-Man stays in the MCU: Another Marvel movie planned
At the end of August it was announced that Sony and Marvel will go their separate ways. Tom Holland's Spider-Man would therefore no longer be part of the "Avengers" universe. It was completely unclear how the films about Tom Holland should proceed.
Now the surprise: Spider-Man receives after "Spider-Man: Homecoming" and "Spider-Man: Far From Home" another final third film within the Marvel Cinematic Universe (MCU). This is according to several U.S. mediaries. Accordingly, the theatrical release should already be fixed: In the U.S
Marvel boos Kevin Feige is quoted as saying: “I’m delighted that Spidey’s path will continue in the MCU. Me and everyone at the Marvel studios is very pleased that we can continue to work on ... He’s the only superhero with the ability to play in different universes as Sony continues to work on his Spidey verse. You never know what the future will bring.”
But how does the sudden good news come about? Allegedly, Sony Marvel is said to have submitted an offer. According to industry magazines, Disney, to which Marvel belongs, would participate with 30 percent in the revenues of coming Spider-Man movies. However, the news is not confirmed.
That's how Duchess Meghan and Prince Harry show up in the 'Suits' finale
After nine seasons, the series "Suits" in the USA has now reached the grand finale. In the last episode, there were many happy endings and just as many cameo appearances. And also the royal couple Prince Harry (35) and his Meghan (38), who participated in the series for many years, paid tribute in a charming way. Attention, the following sections contain spoilers to the series finale.
One thing ahead: Duchess Meghan did not return in the finale for a final farewell salute as Rachel Zane, at least not for new scenes. Instead, some retrospectives showed once again the most memorable scenes with today's Duchess. For example, the moments of the failed and successful wedding to Mike or how she greets him after his suspended prison sentence.
But also her real husband, Prince Harry, was mentioned casually in the "Suits" finale, of course, in a wedding scene. When Louis Litt (Rick Hoffman, 49) walks straight towards the altar to marry his fiancée Sheila (Rachael Harris, 51), Harvey Specter (Gabriel Macht, 47) makes a telling remark regarding his suit.
Aegean: Baby and four other children drown in boating accident
A baby and four other children drowned capsizing a refugee boat in the Aegean. A total of seven people died in the accident, as the Greek coast guard announced on Friday. The nationalities are still unclear.
The boat sank near the Greek island of Inousses, located between the island of Chios and the Turkish mainland. Four children, three women and five men could be rescued.
Hundreds of migrants from Turkey are currently converting to the Greek islands in the east of the Aegean. Last year, 174 people were killed on this route, according to the United Nations Refugee Agency (UNHCR). German Interior Minister Horst Seehofer has now announced that he intends to visit both countries.
Together with his French colleague and the responsible EU commissioner, he wanted to "inform himself in Turkey about what it is like about the refugee situation," Seehofer said on Friday. He then wanted to learn more about the "administrative problems of the Greeks" in Athens in order to be able to help cope.
A spokeswoman for the EU Commission confirmed Seehofer's planned trip on Friday with EU Interior Commissioner Dimitris Avramopoulos and France's Interior Minister Christophe Castaner.
In Greece, an unusual number of migrants continue to arrive from Turkey. On Tuesday alone, 654 people transferred to the Greek islands, the Greek civil protection ministry said on Wednesday.
Is the Turkey deal still working?
The increased numbers most recently raised doubts about the functioning of the EU-Turkey agreement concluded in 2016. This stipulates that the EU can send back all refugees and migrants illegally coming to the Greek islands via Turkey. In return, the EU wanted to accept a Syrian refugee from Turkey for every deported Syrian.
However, the processing of asylum applications is progressing painstakingly on the Greek side due to staff shortages. According to the Ministry of Civil Protection, almost 29 400 people are waiting for Lesbos, Chios, Samos, Leros and Kos, according to the Ministry of Civil Protection. The worst situation is on Lesbos: 5200 migrants are currently housed in a camp with capacities for 650 people.
Never since the EU-Turkish Refugee Pact came into force in March 2016, there have been so many.
Climate change: Heat leads to more heart attacks
According to physicians, climate change will put the health system to the test in the future. The climate changes are "certainly" one of the biggest challenges for the health system in the next 20 to 50 years, said Andreas Zeiher, president of the German Society for Cardiology. Above all, the cardiovascular system caused high temperatures and large temperature jumps within a short period of time and increasingly led to heart attacks. Especially people with cardiovascular diseases
Green politician has to answer for embarrassing photo breakdown
The head of the Canadian Greens, Elizabeth May, in the election campaign - was for this reason the mug manipulated?
The Canadian Green Party leader holds a reusable cup in her hand in a photo. But now it came out: The cup was manipulated.
Ottawa/Canada - It has gone unnoticed for quite a long time - and in the end noticed. The reusable coffee mug with a metal straw, Elizabeth May, the head of the Canadian Greens, has never held in her hand. On the party's website she was seen in a photo with this cup, in conversation with citizens. However, the photo had already been published before and also spread to the media
Greens leader has to answer for photo breakdown
The National Post then accused May of manipulation and that the party produces fake news in order to appear more believable on the topic of "environmental protection". And this in the middle of an election campaign to the lower house election, in whice Trudeau is increasingly difficult because of a bribery scandal.
The photo went viral on social media and sparked the discussion about fake news. A user shares a juxtaposition of the photos and writes: "The Greens manipulate a photo of their chairmen to hide their disposable cup".
May was shocked and insisted on her blog that she knew nothing about this action. In order to underline this, she immediately posted the original: she, in conversation with citizens, a mug in her hand. However, he correctly, she was made of bamboo. She always had it with her. A spokeswoman for the party said that it was only a matter of housing the party logo in the picture, and not to cover something away
"I don't want to call him stupid, but there was nothing to hide," she tells the Guardian and asks for something from her over-engaged team: "If they want to edit a picture again, they wouldn't be able to do something with my face, so that I look younger?"
The German Greens have also fallen into shitstorms because of their use of disposable plastics or because of air travel - but their pictures were not photo-shopped. So the head of the Bavarian Greens, Katharina Schulze, ate ice cream with plastic spoon on holiday, as merkur.de* reported.
Influencer wants free upgrade: Lifelong flight ban at Cathay
Jaqueline Ng was allegedly assured of an upgrade by Cathay Paciface, but when she asked for it at check-in, she was not allowed on board - and she should never be allowed to fly with Cathay again.
Spontaneously get an upgrade for business class - that's what many passengers dream of. Sometimes they're lucky, and the employees at check-in offer a change from economy class for free.
However, this case shows how you shouldn’t do it: A self-explained influencer has actively asked the airline Cathay Pacific for a free upgrade. Jaqueline Ng follows more than 65,000 users on Instagram. On May 30, she flew back from Taiwan to New York and on June 7.
On the outbound flight, the employees denied Jaqueline an upgrade, even though she presented an e-mail in which Cathay Pacific allegedly put an upheaval. The letter is said to have been received by the influencer from a marketing employee of the airline. The airline, on the other hand, accuses Jaqueline of falsifying the mail.
On the return flight, Jaqueline tried again to get an upgrade for the long-haul flight. This time, the woman is said to have shown the same mail, but with a different reference number. The employees at JFK airport were skeptical: They checked at the headquarters. And in fact, the employee from whom the e-mail supposedly came is said to have denied the mail contact with Jaqueline.
Airline employees at the airport canceled Jaqueline's ticket and imposed a lifetime flight ban with Cathay Pacific. For 1,400 US dollars (around 1,280 euros), she had to buy herself a new flight ticket to Taiwan.
With her case, the influencer turned to Elliot Advocacy, a company that represents consumer interests, because Cathay had reimbursed her the price for her original ticket, but Jaqueline also demanded back the cost of the new ticket.
But as long as Jaqueline does not submit the metaries of the mail, the case remains clear: For Cathay, the influencer has committed fraud, she remains blocked for future flights. In addition, the airline’s terms and conditions state that it does not have to pay for the new flight. Jaqueline does not want to be satisfied with this – and is now joining a lawyer.
Oil prices give way after report of Yemen ceasefire
Oil prices fell by more than a dollar on Friday noon. Most recently, a barrel (159 liters) of the North Sea variety Brent costed US$61.83 - around 1.4 percent less than the previous day. The price for a barrel of the American variety West Texas Intermediate (WTI) felled 1.3 percent to 55.77 dollars.
The trigger for the price slide was a report by the Wall Street Journal about a ceasefire in Yemen. Saudi Arabia is said to have agreed to a ceasefire in four areas.
A drone attack on the oil facilities in Saudi Arabia had brought five percent of the world’s oil production to a halt in mid-September. The Houthi rebels allied with Iran in the civil war-torn country of Yemen had claimed the attacks for themselves. Saudi Arabia, like the U.S. government, blames Iran. A ceasefire would defuse political tension in the region.
If an agreement is actually reached, one could focus on growth and demand worries, Ole Hansen, head of the raw materials strategy department at Saxo Bank told Bloomberg news agency.
Oil prices are thus continuing its downward movement of the past few days. A week ago, a barrel of WTI oil had still cost up to almost 59 dollars, Brent almost 65 dollars.
Bloomberg had recently reported, citing insiders, that the repair work on oil facilities in Saudia is progressing faster than expected after the attack in mid-September. Accordingly, the production volume has again reached a volume of eight million barrels per day and is moving back towards the original quantity.
Recently, there have been press releases on several occasions, citing insiders who report rapid advances by the state-owned oil company Saudi Aramco in the repair work, but commodity experts pointed out that Saudi Aramco is currently planning an IPO and wants to avoid negative headlines.
At the same time, there was some relaxation in the conflict between Iran and Western countries: The British oil tanker "Stena Impero" is more than two months after being arrested by Iran and the recently granted clearance on the way to leave the port. The crew is now preparing the departure while the ship is anchored, shipping company chief Erik Hånell told the Swedish radio in a text message on Friday morning.
However, the U.S. announced that it was relocating missiles and soldiers to the Iranian nemesis to Saudi Arabia. The fear of an escalation between the Islamic Republic and countries such as the U.S. and Britain had pushed oil prices up sharply at times in recent weeks.
Accusations against Trump intensify
In the affair over possible abuse of power by U.S. President Donal Donald Trump, cover-up allegations against the White House have become loud. Several leading government employees have made intensive efforts, after a contentious phone call between Trump and Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky in late July to prevent access to the wording version of the conversation. This was explained by an anonymous whistlebloor. The speaker of the House of Representatives, the Democrat Nancy Pelos
North Korea thinks third summit of Trump and Kim is questionable
North Korea has raised doubts that there will soon come to a third summit by ruler Kim Jong-un and U.S. President Donald Trump in the nuclear dispute. The sense and chances of such a meeting are questionable because of a lack of progress in implementing agreements from the first summit in Singapore, top diplomat Kim Kye-gwan said on Friday, according to agency KCNA.
So far, the U.S. has done nothing to implement last year’s summit’s resolutions. He also criticized intensified U.S. sanctuaries and the joint U.S.-Southear military maneuvers. He hopes, however, a “wise” and “brave” decision by Trump so that a rapprochement comes again.
Trump had said on Monday that he wanted to know what a third summit with Kim should bring before he agreed. The negotiations for a disarmament of the North Korean nuclear and missile program have stalled since the failed second summit of Trump and Kim in February in Hanoi. In June, both then met in the demilitarized zone between the two Korean states.
