Pound under pressure - interest rate cut expected
British pound under pressure
In the face of an imminent rate cut, the pound loses value against the euro.
Frankfurt Speculations on an imminent interest rate cut by the Bank of England (BoE) put the pound sterling. The British currency cheapened by up to 0.4 per cent to 1.2269 dollars on Friday.
The sales were sparked by Michael Saunder’s comments, which brought an interest rate cut, even if a chaotic Brexit could be averted, as uncertainty would affect the British economy for the foreseeable future. This statement is surprising, since Saunders is considered an advocate of a restrictive monetary policy, said analyst Ricardo Evangelista of the brokerage house ActivTrades.
Last week, the Bank of England had already pointed to the strains on the economy caused by the Brexit hickhack, without directly signalling an interest rate cut. Prime Minister Boris Johnson wants to lead his country out of the EU if necessary even without a divorce agreement on October 31.
More: With his provocations, the British Prime Minister has taken a high risk, but it could pay off for him, says Handelsblatt editor Kerstin Leitel.
Doping: Further Russian manipulations proven
Material from the Moscow analysis laboratory is manipulated - this is confirmed by David Howman, former director of the World Anti-Doping Agency (Wada) and today's head of integrity of the World Athletics Federation.
At the start of the World Athletics Championships, new details about the doping scandal in Russia are revealed - and they are destroying the little in terms of credibility that Russian sport had regained at the beginning of the year and only partially. In January 2019, the Moscow doping lab finally delivered the required rehearsals from 2012 to 2015 to the World Anti-Doping Agency (Wada).
In December 2014, systematic fraud had been revealed in Russian sport. On this, the Wada had banned the Russian Anti-Doping Agency (Rusada) for three years. During this time, Russian athletes were not completely banned from the world stage. After individual examination at the 2016 Summer Games in Rio, around 270 athletes of the country were allowed to compete under a neutral flag
Positive findings are missing
The following September, Wada revoked Rusada's ban, but demanded access to the laboratory and the said samples. The data were intended to provide information on the scope of cover-up of doping cases in state doping. They were intended to help subsequently punish athletes. Three months later, they were finally delivered.
However, the chairman of the independent Integrity Commission (AIU) of the World Athletics Federation (IAAF) has now confirmed that the doping data from the Moscow analysis laboratory has been manipulated. "Yes. We received the data nine months ago. We should check whether the task force has received the correct data," David Howman said in an interview of the "Frankfurter Allgemeine Zeitung" from Friday.
World Cup not affected
This confirms IAAF’s stance: the Athletics Federation is the only organization to hold on to Russia’s ban. At the World Cup in Doha, 30 Russian athletes compete under a neutral flag. They had to prove that they train outside the Russian system and undergo tests.
At the beginning of this week, Wada had already put manipulations in the room and urged Russia to respond to the accusations within three weeks. Russia is now threatening the renewed suspension of Rusada and, in the worst-case case, also the exclusion from the Olympic Games 2020 in Tokyo.
